    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Matter, States of Matter and Kinetic Theory',
    'body': 'Chemistry begins with matter. Matter is anything that has mass and occupies space, and every substance a WAEC candidate studies — from the oxygen in air to the sodium chloride in a laboratory bottle — is matter. Matter is classified first into pure substances and mixtures. Pure substances are either elements (made of only one kind of atom, such as copper, oxygen or sulphur) or compounds (two or more elements chemically combined in a fixed ratio, such as water, H2O, or calcium trioxocarbonate(IV), CaCO3). Mixtures contain two or more substances physically combined in any proportion, and their components retain their individual properties; air, brass, soil and palm wine are common examples. A homogeneous mixture (a solution) has a uniform composition throughout, while a heterogeneous mixture such as sand in water does not.\n\nMatter exists in three common physical states: solid, liquid and gas. In a solid, particles are closely packed in a regular, fixed arrangement and can only vibrate about fixed positions; solids therefore have a definite shape and a definite volume, and they are almost incompressible. In a liquid, particles are still close together but are free to slide over one another, so a liquid has a definite volume but takes the shape of its container. In a gas, particles are far apart, move rapidly and randomly in all directions, and there are negligible forces of attraction between them; a gas therefore has neither definite shape nor definite volume and is easily compressed. A fourth state, plasma, exists at extremely high temperatures where atoms are ionised, but WAEC syllabus questions concentrate on the first three.\n\nThe kinetic theory of matter explains these observations. Its assumptions are: all matter is made of tiny discrete particles (atoms, molecules or ions); these particles are in constant motion; the kinetic energy of the particles increases with temperature; and particles attract one another with forces that weaken as the distance between them increases. The theory neatly explains diffusion (the movement of particles from a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration, faster in gases than in liquids), Brownian motion (the random zig-zag movement of smoke particles seen under a microscope, caused by bombardment from invisible air molecules), evaporation, and gas pressure (caused by the continuous bombardment of container walls by moving molecules).\n\nChanges of state are physical changes and are reversible. Melting is solid to liquid at the melting point; freezing is the reverse; vaporisation or boiling is liquid to gas at the boiling point; condensation is gas to liquid; and sublimation is the direct change of a solid to a gas without passing through the liquid state, shown by ammonium chloride, iodine, solid carbon(IV) oxide and anhydrous aluminium chloride. During a change of state the temperature remains constant even though heat is still supplied; this heat, called latent heat, is used to overcome the forces of attraction between particles rather than to raise kinetic energy. A heating curve therefore shows two horizontal plateaux.\n\nA physical change involves no new substance and is easily reversed (dissolving salt, melting ice, magnetising iron). A chemical change produces a new substance, is usually accompanied by an energy change, and is not easily reversed (burning of magnesium, rusting of iron, souring of milk). In a WAEC practical context, Oyedele may be asked to distinguish the two by observing colour change, gas evolution, permanent mass change or heat effects.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: A student heats a solid and records that the temperature rises steadily, then remains constant at 80 degrees Celsius for six minutes before rising again. Explain the constant temperature.\nSolution: At 80 degrees Celsius the solid is melting, so 80 degrees Celsius is its melting point. The heat supplied during that period is latent heat of fusion and is used to weaken and break the forces of attraction holding the particles in their fixed lattice positions. Since the heat does not increase the average kinetic energy of the particles, the thermometer reading does not change. Once all the solid has melted, further heating again raises kinetic energy and the temperature rises.\n\nExample 2: Explain why a gas exerts pressure on the walls of its container and why the pressure increases when the gas is heated at constant volume.\nSolution: Gas molecules move rapidly and randomly, and they continually collide with the walls of the container. Each collision exerts a small force, and the total force per unit area is the gas pressure. When the gas is heated, the average kinetic energy of the molecules increases, so they move faster, strike the walls more frequently and with greater force. At constant volume this greater rate and force of bombardment produces a higher pressure.\n\nExample 3: Ammonia gas and hydrogen chloride gas are released at opposite ends of a long dry glass tube. A white ring forms nearer the hydrogen chloride end. Explain.\nSolution: The two gases diffuse towards each other and react to form ammonium chloride, NH4Cl, seen as a white smoke ring. Ammonia has a relative molecular mass of 17 while hydrogen chloride has 36.5, so by Graham\'s law of diffusion the lighter ammonia diffuses faster. Ammonia therefore travels a greater distance in the same time, and the ring forms closer to the hydrogen chloride end of the tube.\n\nExample 4: Classify each of the following as element, compound or mixture: brass, sulphur, sodium trioxonitrate(V), air, and iron filings mixed with sulphur.\nSolution: Sulphur is an element because it contains only one type of atom. Sodium trioxonitrate(V), NaNO3, is a compound because different elements are chemically combined in a fixed ratio. Brass is a mixture (an alloy of copper and zinc), air is a mixture of gases in variable proportions, and iron filings mixed with sulphur is a mixture because the iron can still be removed with a magnet.\n\nExample 5: State three pieces of evidence from everyday life that support the kinetic theory of matter.\nSolution: First, the smell of frying food spreads through a house, showing that particles move and diffuse. Second, a drop of ink placed in still water gradually colours the whole beaker, showing particle motion in liquids. Third, Brownian motion of smoke particles under a microscope shows that invisible air molecules are moving and colliding with the visible particles. Each observation is only explainable if matter is particulate and in constant motion.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Matter is anything that has mass and occupies space.\n- Matter is classified into pure substances (elements and compounds) and mixtures.\n- An element contains only one kind of atom and cannot be split by chemical means.\n- A compound contains two or more elements chemically combined in a fixed ratio by mass.\n- A mixture contains substances physically combined in any proportion.\n- Components of a mixture retain their individual chemical properties.\n- Homogeneous mixtures have uniform composition; heterogeneous mixtures do not.\n- Solids have fixed shape and fixed volume because particles occupy fixed lattice positions.\n- Liquids have fixed volume but no fixed shape because particles slide over one another.\n- Gases have neither fixed shape nor fixed volume and are easily compressed.\n- The kinetic theory states that matter is made of tiny particles in constant motion.\n- Average kinetic energy of particles is directly proportional to absolute temperature.\n- Diffusion is the movement of particles from higher to lower concentration.\n- Diffusion is fastest in gases and slowest in solids.\n- Graham\'s law states that the rate of diffusion is inversely proportional to the square root of density or relative molecular mass.\n- Brownian motion is direct evidence of the random motion of fluid particles.\n- Gas pressure arises from bombardment of container walls by moving molecules.\n- Melting point is the temperature at which a solid changes to a liquid.\n- Boiling point is the temperature at which the vapour pressure of a liquid equals atmospheric pressure.\n- Temperature stays constant during a change of state because latent heat is absorbed.\n- Latent heat of fusion breaks the forces holding a solid lattice together.\n- Latent heat of vaporisation separates liquid particles completely.\n- Sublimation is the direct change of solid to vapour on heating.\n- Substances that sublime include iodine, ammonium chloride and solid carbon(IV) oxide.\n- Evaporation occurs at all temperatures, but boiling occurs only at the boiling point.\n- Evaporation is increased by higher temperature, larger surface area and moving air.\n- Physical changes produce no new substance and are easily reversible.\n- Chemical changes produce new substances and are usually accompanied by energy changes.\n- Rusting, burning and fermentation are chemical changes.\n- Melting, dissolving and magnetising are physical changes.\n- Impurities lower the melting point and raise the boiling point of a substance.\n- A pure substance melts and boils sharply at a fixed temperature.\n- Heating and cooling curves show plateaux at the melting and boiling points.\n- Understanding kinetic theory underpins later topics such as gas laws and reaction rates.',
