    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Atomic Structure',
    'body': 'The atom is the smallest particle of an element that can take part in a chemical reaction. Although Dalton originally described the atom as indivisible, later experiments showed that it is made up of smaller subatomic particles. Thomson discovered the electron and proposed the plum pudding model; Rutherford\'s gold foil experiment, in which a few alpha particles were deflected through large angles, showed that the atom is mostly empty space with a small, dense, positively charged nucleus; Bohr then proposed that electrons move in fixed energy levels or shells around this nucleus; and Chadwick later discovered the neutron.\n\nThere are three fundamental subatomic particles. The proton has a relative mass of 1 and a charge of +1 and is found in the nucleus. The neutron has a relative mass of 1 and no charge and is also found in the nucleus. The electron has a negligible mass of about 1/1840 and a charge of -1, and it moves in shells outside the nucleus. In a neutral atom, the number of protons equals the number of electrons, so the atom has no overall charge. The atomic number, Z, is the number of protons in the nucleus and defines the identity of the element. The mass number, A, is the sum of protons and neutrons, so the number of neutrons equals A minus Z. An atom is represented as A over Z followed by the symbol.\n\nIsotopes are atoms of the same element having the same atomic number but different mass numbers, because they contain different numbers of neutrons. Chlorine exists as chlorine-35 and chlorine-37, carbon exists as carbon-12, carbon-13 and carbon-14, and hydrogen exists as protium, deuterium and tritium. Isotopes have identical chemical properties because chemistry depends on electronic configuration, but they differ in physical properties such as density and rate of diffusion. Relative atomic mass is the weighted average of the masses of the isotopes, taking their percentage abundances into account, which is why chlorine has a relative atomic mass of 35.5 rather than a whole number.\n\nElectrons occupy shells of increasing energy. The maximum number of electrons in a shell is given by 2n squared, so the first shell holds 2, the second 8, the third 18 and the fourth 32, although for the first twenty elements the third shell is filled to 8 before the fourth begins. The electronic configuration of sodium (Z equals 11) is 2, 8, 1; that of chlorine (Z equals 17) is 2, 8, 7; and that of calcium (Z equals 20) is 2, 8, 8, 2. Using the s, p, d notation, sodium is 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s1. Electrons fill orbitals in order of increasing energy following the Aufbau principle; Hund\'s rule requires that orbitals of equal energy are singly occupied before pairing; and the Pauli exclusion principle limits each orbital to two electrons of opposite spin.\n\nThe outermost, or valence, electrons determine the chemical behaviour of an element, its group in the periodic table, and its valency. Atoms react to attain a stable duplet or octet configuration like that of the nearest noble gas. Metals lose valence electrons to form cations, while non-metals gain electrons to form anions. Ionisation energy is the energy required to remove one mole of electrons from one mole of gaseous atoms, and electron affinity is the energy change when a gaseous atom gains an electron.\n\nRadioactivity is the spontaneous disintegration of an unstable nucleus with the emission of alpha particles (helium nuclei), beta particles (fast electrons) or gamma rays (high-energy electromagnetic radiation). Half-life is the time taken for half of a given mass of a radioactive substance to decay. Applications include carbon-14 dating, cobalt-60 in cancer therapy and nuclear power generation, while hazards include radiation burns, mutation and cancer.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: An atom X has mass number 39 and atomic number 19. State the number of protons, neutrons and electrons, and write its electronic configuration.\nSolution: The atomic number 19 means the atom contains 19 protons, and because the atom is neutral it also contains 19 electrons. The number of neutrons equals mass number minus atomic number, that is 39 minus 19, which gives 20 neutrons. Filling the shells gives 2, 8, 8, 1, so X is potassium, a Group 1 metal in Period 4 with valency 1.\n\nExample 2: Chlorine consists of 75 per cent chlorine-35 and 25 per cent chlorine-37. Calculate its relative atomic mass.\nSolution: Relative atomic mass equals the sum of each isotopic mass multiplied by its fractional abundance. This gives (35 times 75/100) plus (37 times 25/100), that is 26.25 plus 9.25. The total is 35.5. This is why chlorine is quoted as 35.5 even though no single chlorine atom has that mass.\n\nExample 3: Explain why isotopes of an element have the same chemical properties but different physical properties.\nSolution: Chemical properties depend on the number and arrangement of electrons, particularly the valence electrons, and all isotopes of an element have the same atomic number and therefore the same electronic configuration. Their reactions with acids, oxygen and other reagents are consequently identical. Physical properties such as density, rate of diffusion and boiling point depend on mass, and isotopes differ in the number of neutrons and hence in mass. This is the principle behind isotope separation by diffusion.\n\nExample 4: A radioactive isotope has a half-life of 8 days. If Toluwani starts with 64 g of the isotope, what mass remains after 32 days?\nSolution: The number of half-lives is 32 divided by 8, which equals 4. After each half-life the mass is halved: 64 g becomes 32 g, then 16 g, then 8 g, then 4 g. Therefore 4 g of the isotope remains after 32 days. The general formula is final mass equals initial mass multiplied by one-half raised to the power of the number of half-lives.\n\nExample 5: Write the electronic configuration of the sulphide ion and explain how it is formed.\nSolution: A sulphur atom has atomic number 16 and configuration 2, 8, 6. To attain the stable octet of argon it gains two electrons rather than losing six, since gaining is energetically easier. The sulphide ion therefore has the configuration 2, 8, 8 and carries a charge of 2 minus, written S2-. The number of protons remains 16, so the ion has more electrons than protons and is negatively charged.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- An atom is the smallest particle of an element that can take part in a chemical change.\n- Protons are positively charged and located in the nucleus.\n- Neutrons are neutral and located in the nucleus.\n- Electrons are negatively charged and occupy shells around the nucleus.\n- Relative mass of proton and neutron is 1; that of an electron is about 1/1840.\n- Atomic number equals the number of protons and identifies the element.\n- Mass number equals the number of protons plus neutrons.\n- Number of neutrons equals mass number minus atomic number.\n- In a neutral atom, protons equal electrons.\n- Rutherford\'s experiment proved the existence of a small dense positive nucleus.\n- Most of the atom is empty space.\n- Bohr proposed that electrons occupy fixed energy levels.\n- Chadwick discovered the neutron.\n- Isotopes have the same atomic number but different mass numbers.\n- Isotopes have identical chemical but different physical properties.\n- Relative atomic mass is the weighted average of isotopic masses.\n- Maximum electrons per shell is given by 2n squared.\n- The first four shells hold 2, 8, 18 and 32 electrons.\n- Aufbau principle: orbitals fill in order of increasing energy.\n- Hund\'s rule: degenerate orbitals are singly filled before pairing.\n- Pauli exclusion principle: an orbital holds at most two electrons of opposite spin.\n- Valence electrons determine chemical properties and group number.\n- Metals lose electrons to form positively charged cations.\n- Non-metals gain electrons to form negatively charged anions.\n- Ionisation energy is energy needed to remove an electron from a gaseous atom.\n- Ionisation energy increases across a period and decreases down a group.\n- Electron affinity is the energy change when a gaseous atom gains an electron.\n- Noble gases are stable because their outer shells are full.\n- Radioactivity is spontaneous nuclear disintegration.\n- Alpha particles are helium nuclei with charge 2 plus.\n- Beta particles are high-speed electrons.\n- Gamma rays are electromagnetic radiation with no mass or charge.\n- Half-life is the time for half of a radioactive sample to decay.\n- Radioisotopes are used in medicine, agriculture, dating and power generation.\n- Excessive exposure to radiation causes burns, mutations and cancer.',
