    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'The Periodic Table',
    'body': 'The periodic table is the arrangement of elements in order of increasing atomic number so that elements with similar chemical properties fall in the same vertical column. Early attempts included Dobereiner\'s triads and Newlands\' law of octaves, while Mendeleev arranged elements by increasing atomic mass and boldly left gaps for undiscovered elements such as germanium, correctly predicting their properties. Moseley later showed that atomic number, not atomic mass, is the true basis of periodicity, and this resolved anomalies such as the placement of argon before potassium. The modern periodic law states that the properties of elements are a periodic function of their atomic numbers.\n\nThe table consists of horizontal rows called periods and vertical columns called groups. The period number equals the number of occupied electron shells, and the group number for main-group elements equals the number of valence electrons. Group 1 elements are the alkali metals, Group 2 the alkaline earth metals, Group 7 the halogens and Group 8 or 0 the noble gases. Between Groups 2 and 3 in Periods 4 and beyond lie the transition elements, which have partially filled d orbitals. Transition metals show variable oxidation states, form coloured ions, act as catalysts, form complex ions and are generally hard, dense metals with high melting points.\n\nPeriodic trends follow logically from electronic structure. Atomic radius decreases across a period because the nuclear charge increases while electrons are added to the same shell, so the outer electrons are pulled in more strongly; it increases down a group because a new shell is added each time and shielding by inner electrons increases. Ionisation energy increases across a period and decreases down a group, for the same reasons in reverse. Electronegativity, the tendency of an atom to attract a shared pair of electrons, increases across a period and decreases down a group, with fluorine the most electronegative element. Metallic character decreases across a period and increases down a group, while non-metallic character does the opposite.\n\nThe chemical consequences are important for WAEC answers. Across Period 3 the oxides change from basic (Na2O, MgO) through amphoteric (Al2O3) to acidic (P4O10, SO3, Cl2O7). Chlorides change from ionic solids that dissolve to give neutral solutions, through those that hydrolyse to give acidic solutions, to covalent, fuming liquids. Down Group 1 reactivity increases because the outer electron is lost more easily, so caesium reacts far more violently with water than lithium. Down Group 7 reactivity decreases because the atom attracts an extra electron less strongly, so fluorine is the most reactive halogen and iodine the least; a more reactive halogen therefore displaces a less reactive one from its salt solution, as when chlorine displaces bromine from potassium bromide.\n\nGroup 1 metals are soft, have low densities and melting points, are stored under oil, react with water to give hydrogen and an alkaline hydroxide, and form ionic compounds with valency one. Group 2 metals are harder, denser and less reactive than their Group 1 neighbours, with valency two. Group 7 elements exist as diatomic molecules, are coloured, are oxidising agents and form halide salts. Group 8 elements are monatomic, chemically inert and used in lamps, welding and balloons.\n\nUnderstanding the table allows a candidate to predict formulae, bonding type, and reactions without memorising every fact. If Oyedele knows that an element has configuration 2, 8, 2 he can immediately state that it is in Group 2 and Period 3, that it is a reactive metal (magnesium), that it forms a 2 plus ion, that its oxide is basic, and that its chloride is MgCl2.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: An element Q has atomic number 15. State its group, period and the formula of its oxide.\nSolution: The electronic configuration of Q is 2, 8, 5, so it has three occupied shells and five valence electrons. It is therefore in Period 3 and Group 5, and it is the non-metal phosphorus. Its common oxide is phosphorus(V) oxide, P4O10, which is acidic and dissolves in water to give trioxophosphoric(V) acid.\n\nExample 2: Explain why the atomic radius of sodium is greater than that of chlorine although both are in Period 3.\nSolution: Both elements have three occupied electron shells, so shielding by inner electrons is essentially the same. Chlorine has 17 protons while sodium has only 11, so chlorine has a much greater effective nuclear charge. This stronger attraction pulls the outer electrons closer to the nucleus, giving chlorine a smaller atomic radius. Atomic radius therefore decreases from left to right across a period.\n\nExample 3: Chlorine water is added to potassium iodide solution. State and explain the observation.\nSolution: The colourless solution turns brown because iodine is liberated. Chlorine is higher in Group 7 and is a stronger oxidising agent, so it removes electrons from iodide ions. The ionic equation is Cl2 plus 2I- gives 2Cl- plus I2. Shaking the mixture with a little kerosene produces a purple organic layer, confirming iodine.\n\nExample 4: Why is argon placed before potassium even though argon has a higher relative atomic mass?\nSolution: In the modern periodic table elements are arranged by increasing atomic number, not atomic mass. Argon has atomic number 18 while potassium has 19, so argon must come first. Placing them by mass would put the inert argon in Group 1 with the reactive alkali metals, which contradicts their chemical behaviour. Moseley\'s work on X-ray spectra established atomic number as the correct ordering property.\n\nExample 5: Give three properties of transition elements that distinguish them from Group 1 metals.\nSolution: Transition elements show variable oxidation states, for example iron as Fe2+ and Fe3+, whereas sodium shows only plus one. They form coloured compounds and coloured ions in solution because of d-orbital electron transitions, while Group 1 compounds are generally white. They also act as catalysts, form complex ions with ligands, and are hard and dense with high melting points, unlike soft, low-melting Group 1 metals.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- The modern periodic law states that properties are a periodic function of atomic number.\n- Mendeleev arranged elements by atomic mass and left gaps for undiscovered elements.\n- Moseley established atomic number as the correct basis of arrangement.\n- Horizontal rows are periods; vertical columns are groups.\n- Period number equals the number of occupied electron shells.\n- Group number of a main-group element equals its number of valence electrons.\n- Group 1 elements are alkali metals with valency one.\n- Group 2 elements are alkaline earth metals with valency two.\n- Group 7 elements are halogens and exist as diatomic molecules.\n- Group 8 or 0 elements are noble gases and are chemically inert.\n- Transition elements have partially filled d orbitals.\n- Transition elements show variable oxidation states and form coloured ions.\n- Transition metals and their compounds are widely used as catalysts.\n- Atomic radius decreases across a period due to increasing nuclear charge.\n- Atomic radius increases down a group due to additional shells.\n- Ionisation energy increases across a period and decreases down a group.\n- Electronegativity increases across a period and decreases down a group.\n- Fluorine is the most electronegative element.\n- Metallic character decreases across a period and increases down a group.\n- Group 1 reactivity increases down the group.\n- Group 7 reactivity decreases down the group.\n- A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive halogen from its salt.\n- Oxides change from basic through amphoteric to acidic across a period.\n- Aluminium oxide and zinc oxide are amphoteric.\n- Sodium oxide dissolves in water to give an alkaline solution.\n- Sulphur and phosphorus oxides dissolve to give acidic solutions.\n- Cations are smaller than their parent atoms.\n- Anions are larger than their parent atoms.\n- Alkali metals are stored under oil to prevent reaction with air and moisture.\n- Alkali metals react with water to give hydrogen and a hydroxide.\n- Halogens are oxidising agents and form halide ions.\n- Noble gases are monatomic and used in lamps, welding and balloons.\n- Elements in the same group have similar chemical properties.\n- Hydrogen is unique and may be placed in Group 1 or Group 7.\n- The periodic table allows prediction of formulae, bonding and reactivity.',
