    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Chemical Bonding',
    'body': 'Chemical bonding is the force of attraction that holds atoms or ions together in a compound. Atoms combine in order to attain the stable electronic configuration of the nearest noble gas, that is a duplet for elements near helium and an octet for most others. The type of bond formed depends on the electronegativity difference between the combining atoms and on whether electrons are transferred or shared.\n\nAn ionic or electrovalent bond is formed by the complete transfer of one or more electrons from a metal atom to a non-metal atom, producing oppositely charged ions held together by strong electrostatic attraction. In sodium chloride, sodium (2, 8, 1) loses one electron to become Na+ with configuration 2, 8, while chlorine (2, 8, 7) gains that electron to become Cl- with configuration 2, 8, 8. Ionic compounds are crystalline solids with high melting and boiling points because a great deal of energy is needed to overcome the strong lattice forces. They are usually soluble in water and other polar solvents, insoluble in organic solvents, and conduct electricity only when molten or in aqueous solution, since the ions must be free to move. Reactions of ionic compounds in solution are fast because they involve free ions.\n\nA covalent bond is formed by the sharing of a pair of electrons between two atoms, each usually contributing one electron. Hydrogen forms H2 with a single bond, oxygen forms O2 with a double bond, and nitrogen forms N2 with a triple bond; water, ammonia and methane are further examples. Covalent compounds usually exist as simple molecules with weak intermolecular forces, so they have low melting and boiling points, are often gases, volatile liquids or soft solids, are generally insoluble in water but soluble in organic solvents, and do not conduct electricity because they contain no free ions. Giant covalent structures such as diamond, graphite and silicon(IV) oxide are exceptions with very high melting points; graphite conducts electricity because each carbon uses only three of its four valence electrons in bonding, leaving delocalised electrons between the layers.\n\nA co-ordinate or dative covalent bond arises when both shared electrons come from the same atom. Examples include the ammonium ion, formed when the lone pair on ammonia is donated to a hydrogen ion, the hydroxonium ion, and complex ions such as the tetraamminecopper(II) ion. Once formed, a dative bond is identical to an ordinary covalent bond.\n\nMetallic bonding is the electrostatic attraction between a lattice of positive metal ions and a sea of delocalised valence electrons. This model explains why metals conduct heat and electricity, are malleable and ductile (layers of ions can slide without breaking the bonding), have lustre, and generally have high melting points and densities.\n\nWeaker intermolecular forces also matter. Van der Waals forces are weak attractions between molecules that increase with molecular size, explaining why the boiling points of the halogens increase down the group. Hydrogen bonding occurs when hydrogen is bonded to a highly electronegative atom such as fluorine, oxygen or nitrogen and is attracted to a lone pair on a neighbouring molecule. Hydrogen bonding accounts for the abnormally high boiling point of water compared with hydrogen sulphide, the lower density of ice than water, the miscibility of ethanol with water, and the structure of proteins.\n\nPolarity arises when bonded atoms have different electronegativities, producing a dipole, as in hydrogen chloride. A molecule may contain polar bonds yet be non-polar overall if the dipoles cancel by symmetry, as in carbon(IV) oxide and tetrachloromethane. Shape follows from electron-pair repulsion: methane is tetrahedral, ammonia pyramidal, water bent or V-shaped, and carbon(IV) oxide linear.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: Using electronic configurations, show the bonding in magnesium chloride and state two properties expected of the compound.\nSolution: Magnesium has configuration 2, 8, 2 and loses two electrons to become Mg2+ with configuration 2, 8. Each chlorine atom, 2, 8, 7, gains one electron to become Cl- with 2, 8, 8, so two chloride ions are required and the formula is MgCl2. The bond is ionic, formed by electron transfer and held by electrostatic attraction. The compound is therefore a crystalline solid with a high melting point that conducts electricity when molten or dissolved in water.\n\nExample 2: Explain why sodium chloride conducts electricity when molten but not when solid.\nSolution: In the solid state the sodium and chloride ions are held rigidly in fixed positions in the giant ionic lattice and cannot move. Electrical conduction in ionic substances requires mobile charge carriers. When the solid is melted the lattice breaks down and the ions become free to move towards the electrodes. The molten salt therefore conducts electricity and is decomposed by it.\n\nExample 3: Water has a much higher boiling point than hydrogen sulphide although sulphur is heavier than oxygen. Explain.\nSolution: Oxygen is much more electronegative than sulphur, so the O-H bond is strongly polar and each water molecule can form hydrogen bonds with neighbouring molecules through the lone pairs on oxygen. These hydrogen bonds are considerably stronger than the weak van der Waals forces between hydrogen sulphide molecules. A large amount of energy is therefore needed to separate water molecules during boiling, giving water a boiling point of 100 degrees Celsius against about minus 60 degrees Celsius for hydrogen sulphide.\n\nExample 4: Describe the formation of the ammonium ion and name the type of bond involved.\nSolution: The nitrogen atom in ammonia has a lone pair of electrons not used in bonding. A hydrogen ion, H+, has no electrons but an empty orbital. The nitrogen donates its lone pair to the hydrogen ion, forming a co-ordinate or dative covalent bond and producing NH4+. All four N-H bonds in the ion are identical in length and strength, and the ion is tetrahedral.\n\nExample 5: Explain, using structure and bonding, why diamond is very hard while graphite is soft and conducts electricity.\nSolution: In diamond each carbon atom is covalently bonded to four others in a rigid three-dimensional tetrahedral network, and breaking the structure requires breaking many strong covalent bonds, so diamond is extremely hard and is used in cutting tools. In graphite each carbon is bonded to only three others in flat hexagonal layers, and the layers are held together by weak van der Waals forces, so they slide over one another and graphite feels slippery and is used as a lubricant. The fourth electron on each carbon is delocalised between the layers, allowing graphite to conduct electricity, which is why it is used for electrodes.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- A chemical bond is the force of attraction holding atoms or ions together.\n- Atoms bond in order to achieve a stable noble gas configuration.\n- Ionic bonding involves complete transfer of electrons from metal to non-metal.\n- Ionic compounds form giant lattices of oppositely charged ions.\n- Ionic compounds have high melting and boiling points.\n- Ionic compounds conduct electricity only when molten or in solution.\n- Ionic compounds are generally soluble in water and insoluble in organic solvents.\n- Covalent bonding involves the sharing of electron pairs.\n- Single, double and triple covalent bonds involve one, two and three shared pairs.\n- Simple molecular covalent compounds have low melting and boiling points.\n- Covalent compounds generally do not conduct electricity.\n- Giant covalent structures include diamond, graphite and silicon(IV) oxide.\n- Diamond is hard because of its rigid three-dimensional covalent network.\n- Graphite conducts electricity because of delocalised electrons between layers.\n- Co-ordinate bonding occurs when one atom supplies both shared electrons.\n- The ammonium and hydroxonium ions contain dative covalent bonds.\n- Metallic bonding involves positive ions in a sea of delocalised electrons.\n- Metallic bonding explains conductivity, malleability, ductility and lustre.\n- Van der Waals forces are weak intermolecular attractions.\n- Van der Waals forces increase with increasing molecular size.\n- Hydrogen bonding occurs when H is bonded to F, O or N.\n- Hydrogen bonding explains the high boiling point of water.\n- Ice is less dense than water because hydrogen bonds create an open structure.\n- Hydrogen bonding makes ethanol and ammonia very soluble in water.\n- Electronegativity difference determines whether a bond is ionic or covalent.\n- Large electronegativity difference favours ionic bonding.\n- A polar bond has an unequal sharing of electrons and a dipole.\n- A symmetrical molecule with polar bonds may still be non-polar overall.\n- Methane is tetrahedral with bond angles of about 109.5 degrees.\n- Ammonia is pyramidal because of one lone pair on nitrogen.\n- Water is bent or V-shaped because of two lone pairs on oxygen.\n- Carbon(IV) oxide is linear and non-polar.\n- Lone pairs repel more strongly than bonding pairs, reducing bond angles.\n- Bond type strongly influences solubility, conductivity and volatility.\n- Predicting bond type from position in the periodic table is a common WAEC skill.',
