    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Chemical Formulae, Equations and the Mole Concept',
    'body': 'Chemical symbols, formulae and equations are the language of chemistry, and the mole concept is the bridge between the microscopic world of atoms and the measurable world of the laboratory. A chemical formula shows the elements present in a compound and the ratio in which their atoms combine. An empirical formula shows the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms, while a molecular formula shows the actual number of atoms of each element in one molecule. Benzene has the empirical formula CH and the molecular formula C6H6; glucose has the empirical formula CH2O and molecular formula C6H12O6. The formula of an ionic compound is obtained by balancing the charges of its ions, so aluminium and sulphate ions give Al2(SO4)3.\n\nThe mole is the amount of substance that contains as many elementary entities as there are atoms in 12 g of carbon-12. That number, 6.02 times 10 to the power 23, is the Avogadro constant. One mole of any substance therefore contains 6.02 times 10 to the 23 particles, and its mass in grams is numerically equal to its relative atomic or relative molecular mass, called the molar mass. One mole of any gas occupies 22.4 dm3 at standard temperature and pressure (0 degrees Celsius and 1 atmosphere) and 24 dm3 at room temperature.\n\nThe central relationships are: number of moles equals mass divided by molar mass; number of moles equals number of particles divided by the Avogadro constant; number of moles of a gas equals volume divided by molar volume; and for solutions, number of moles equals concentration in mol per dm3 multiplied by volume in dm3. Concentration in g per dm3 equals molar concentration multiplied by molar mass.\n\nPercentage composition by mass is calculated as the mass of the element in one mole of compound divided by the molar mass of the compound, multiplied by 100. Empirical formulae are found by dividing the mass or percentage of each element by its relative atomic mass to get moles, then dividing through by the smallest value, and finally multiplying to clear fractions. The molecular formula is a whole-number multiple of the empirical formula, found by dividing the relative molecular mass by the empirical formula mass.\n\nA chemical equation must be balanced because matter is neither created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction; the law of conservation of mass requires the same number of atoms of each element on both sides. State symbols (s), (l), (g) and (aq) should be included. Ionic equations show only the species that actually change, omitting spectator ions; for example, the reaction between silver trioxonitrate(V) and sodium chloride reduces to Ag+ plus Cl- gives AgCl(s).\n\nStoichiometry uses the balanced equation to relate quantities of reactants and products. The coefficients give the mole ratio, from which masses, volumes of gases and volumes of solutions can be calculated. A limiting reactant is the one completely used up, and it determines the maximum yield. Percentage yield equals actual yield divided by theoretical yield multiplied by 100, and losses arise from side reactions, incomplete reaction and transfer losses. In volumetric analysis the titration formula CaVa over CbVb equals na over nb links the concentrations and volumes of acid and base to their stoichiometric ratio.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: Calculate the number of moles in 20 g of calcium trioxocarbonate(IV), CaCO3. (Ca equals 40, C equals 12, O equals 16.)\nSolution: The molar mass of CaCO3 is 40 plus 12 plus (3 times 16), which equals 40 plus 12 plus 48, giving 100 g per mole. Number of moles equals mass divided by molar mass, that is 20 divided by 100. This gives 0.2 mol. This sample therefore contains 0.2 times 6.02 times 10 to the 23, about 1.2 times 10 to the 23 formula units.\n\nExample 2: A compound contains 40 per cent carbon, 6.7 per cent hydrogen and 53.3 per cent oxygen by mass. Its relative molecular mass is 180. Find its empirical and molecular formulae.\nSolution: Dividing each percentage by the relative atomic mass gives 40/12 equals 3.33 for carbon, 6.7/1 equals 6.7 for hydrogen and 53.3/16 equals 3.33 for oxygen. Dividing through by the smallest value, 3.33, gives a ratio of 1 to 2 to 1, so the empirical formula is CH2O with an empirical formula mass of 30. Dividing 180 by 30 gives 6, so the molecular formula is C6H12O6, which is glucose.\n\nExample 3: What volume of carbon(IV) oxide, measured at s.t.p., is produced when 25 g of calcium trioxocarbonate(IV) is completely decomposed?\nSolution: The equation is CaCO3(s) gives CaO(s) plus CO2(g), a 1 to 1 mole ratio. Moles of CaCO3 equal 25 divided by 100, which is 0.25 mol, so 0.25 mol of CO2 is formed. Volume equals moles multiplied by molar volume, that is 0.25 times 22.4 dm3. The volume of carbon(IV) oxide is therefore 5.6 dm3 at s.t.p.\n\nExample 4: 25.0 cm3 of sodium hydroxide solution required 20.0 cm3 of 0.1 mol per dm3 hydrochloric acid for complete neutralisation. Calculate the concentration of the base in mol per dm3 and in g per dm3.\nSolution: The equation NaOH plus HCl gives NaCl plus H2O shows a 1 to 1 ratio. Moles of acid equal 0.1 times 20.0/1000, which is 0.002 mol, so moles of base are also 0.002 mol. Concentration of base equals 0.002 divided by 25.0/1000, giving 0.08 mol per dm3. Multiplying by the molar mass of NaOH, 40, gives 3.2 g per dm3.\n\nExample 5: Balance the equation for the reaction between aluminium and dilute tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid and calculate the mass of aluminium needed to produce 6 dm3 of hydrogen at room temperature (Al equals 27, molar volume 24 dm3).\nSolution: The balanced equation is 2Al plus 3H2SO4 gives Al2(SO4)3 plus 3H2. Moles of hydrogen equal 6 divided by 24, which is 0.25 mol. From the equation 3 mol of hydrogen require 2 mol of aluminium, so 0.25 mol requires 0.25 times 2/3, which is 0.1667 mol of aluminium. Mass equals 0.1667 times 27, giving about 4.5 g of aluminium.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- A chemical formula shows the elements present and the ratio of their atoms.\n- Empirical formula gives the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms.\n- Molecular formula gives the actual number of atoms in one molecule.\n- Molecular formula is a whole-number multiple of the empirical formula.\n- Formulae of ionic compounds are obtained by balancing ionic charges.\n- The mole is the amount of substance containing 6.02 times 10 to the 23 particles.\n- The Avogadro constant is 6.02 times 10 to the power 23 per mole.\n- Molar mass in grams is numerically equal to relative molecular mass.\n- Moles equal mass divided by molar mass.\n- Moles equal number of particles divided by the Avogadro constant.\n- One mole of any gas occupies 22.4 dm3 at s.t.p.\n- One mole of any gas occupies about 24 dm3 at room temperature.\n- Standard temperature and pressure are 273 K and 760 mmHg.\n- Moles in solution equal concentration multiplied by volume in dm3.\n- Concentration in g per dm3 equals molarity multiplied by molar mass.\n- Percentage composition equals mass of element over molar mass times 100.\n- The law of conservation of mass requires balanced equations.\n- State symbols s, l, g and aq should appear in full equations.\n- Ionic equations omit spectator ions.\n- Coefficients in an equation give the mole ratio of reactants and products.\n- The limiting reactant determines the maximum possible yield.\n- Percentage yield equals actual over theoretical yield times 100.\n- Yields are reduced by side reactions and losses during transfer.\n- Avogadro\'s law states equal volumes of gases contain equal numbers of molecules.\n- The law of definite proportions states a compound always has the same composition.\n- The law of multiple proportions applies when elements form more than one compound.\n- Relative molecular mass is the sum of relative atomic masses in a formula.\n- Volumetric analysis uses CaVa over CbVb equals na over nb.\n- A standard solution has an accurately known concentration.\n- Primary standards such as anhydrous sodium trioxocarbonate(IV) are pure and stable.\n- Water of crystallisation must be included in molar mass calculations.\n- Molar mass of hydrated copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) includes five water molecules.\n- Gas volume ratios in reactions equal mole ratios at the same conditions.\n- Always check that atoms and charges balance on both sides of an equation.\n- Mole calculations underpin titration, gas law and electrolysis questions.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
