    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Gas Laws',
    'body': 'The gas laws describe how the volume of a fixed mass of gas responds to changes in pressure and temperature, and they follow directly from the kinetic theory. Because gas particles are far apart and in rapid random motion, their volume is determined almost entirely by the container and by the energy of the molecules rather than by the size of the molecules themselves.\n\nBoyle\'s law states that at constant temperature the volume of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its pressure, that is PV equals a constant, or P1V1 equals P2V2. A graph of V against P is a curve (a rectangular hyperbola), while V against 1 over P is a straight line through the origin. Kinetically, compressing a gas into a smaller volume means the molecules strike the walls more frequently, so pressure rises.\n\nCharles\' law states that at constant pressure the volume of a fixed mass of gas is directly proportional to its absolute temperature, that is V over T equals a constant, or V1 over T1 equals V2 over T2. Temperature must always be converted to kelvin by adding 273 to the Celsius value. A graph of V against temperature in degrees Celsius is a straight line that extrapolates to zero volume at minus 273 degrees Celsius, defining absolute zero, the temperature at which molecular motion theoretically ceases.\n\nThe pressure law, sometimes called Gay-Lussac\'s law, states that at constant volume the pressure of a fixed mass of gas is directly proportional to its absolute temperature, P1 over T1 equals P2 over T2. Combining the three gives the general gas equation, P1V1 over T1 equals P2V2 over T2, which is the most useful single relation for WAEC calculations and is used to convert measured gas volumes to standard temperature and pressure. Introducing the mole gives the ideal gas equation PV equals nRT, where R is the molar gas constant, 8.31 J per mol per kelvin when pressure is in pascals and volume in cubic metres.\n\nDalton\'s law of partial pressures states that the total pressure of a mixture of gases that do not react is the sum of the partial pressures of the individual gases. This matters when a gas is collected over water, because the measured pressure includes the saturated vapour pressure of water; the pressure of the dry gas equals the total pressure minus the vapour pressure of water at that temperature.\n\nGraham\'s law of diffusion states that at constant temperature and pressure the rate of diffusion of a gas is inversely proportional to the square root of its density, or of its relative molecular mass. Therefore R1 over R2 equals the square root of M2 over M1. Light gases such as hydrogen and ammonia diffuse quickly, while heavy gases such as chlorine and carbon(IV) oxide diffuse slowly.\n\nGay-Lussac\'s law of combining volumes states that when gases react they do so in volumes that bear a simple whole-number ratio to one another and to the volume of the gaseous product, all volumes measured at the same temperature and pressure. Avogadro\'s law states that equal volumes of all gases under the same conditions contain equal numbers of molecules, which explains Gay-Lussac\'s observation and allows gas-volume problems to be solved directly from the balanced equation.\n\nReal gases deviate from ideal behaviour at high pressure and low temperature because molecules then occupy an appreciable fraction of the volume and intermolecular attractions are no longer negligible. A gas behaves most ideally at high temperature and low pressure.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: A gas occupies 250 cm3 at a pressure of 750 mmHg. What volume will it occupy at 500 mmHg if the temperature stays constant?\nSolution: This is Boyle\'s law, so P1V1 equals P2V2. Substituting gives 750 times 250 equals 500 times V2, so V2 equals 187500 divided by 500. The new volume is 375 cm3. The volume increases because the pressure has been reduced, as expected from an inverse relationship.\n\nExample 2: A sample of gas has a volume of 400 cm3 at 27 degrees Celsius. What is its volume at 127 degrees Celsius at constant pressure?\nSolution: Convert temperatures to kelvin: 27 plus 273 equals 300 K and 127 plus 273 equals 400 K. Using Charles\' law, V1 over T1 equals V2 over T2, so 400 over 300 equals V2 over 400. Therefore V2 equals (400 times 400) divided by 300, which is about 533.3 cm3.\n\nExample 3: A gas occupies 300 cm3 at 27 degrees Celsius and 700 mmHg. Calculate its volume at s.t.p.\nSolution: Standard conditions are 273 K and 760 mmHg. Using the general gas equation, P1V1 over T1 equals P2V2 over T2, we substitute (700 times 300) over 300 equals (760 times V2) over 273. The left side gives 700, so 760 V2 equals 700 times 273, that is 191100. Dividing gives V2 equal to about 251.4 cm3.\n\nExample 4: Compare the rates of diffusion of hydrogen and oxygen. (H equals 1, O equals 16.)\nSolution: The relative molecular mass of hydrogen is 2 and that of oxygen is 32. By Graham\'s law the rate ratio equals the square root of 32 divided by 2, that is the square root of 16, which is 4. Hydrogen therefore diffuses four times as fast as oxygen under the same conditions. In a diffusion experiment hydrogen would travel four times the distance of oxygen in the same time.\n\nExample 5: 20 cm3 of methane is burnt in excess oxygen. Calculate the volume of carbon(IV) oxide produced at the same temperature and pressure.\nSolution: The equation is CH4(g) plus 2O2(g) gives CO2(g) plus 2H2O(l). By Avogadro\'s law volumes of gases are in the same ratio as moles, and the ratio of methane to carbon(IV) oxide is 1 to 1. Therefore 20 cm3 of methane gives 20 cm3 of carbon(IV) oxide. The water formed is a liquid at room temperature and contributes no gas volume.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Gas laws apply to a fixed mass of gas.\n- Boyle\'s law: volume is inversely proportional to pressure at constant temperature.\n- Boyle\'s law is expressed as P1V1 equals P2V2.\n- A graph of V against 1 over P is a straight line through the origin.\n- Charles\' law: volume is directly proportional to absolute temperature at constant pressure.\n- Charles\' law is expressed as V1 over T1 equals V2 over T2.\n- Temperatures in gas calculations must be in kelvin.\n- Kelvin temperature equals Celsius temperature plus 273.\n- Absolute zero is minus 273 degrees Celsius, or 0 K.\n- At absolute zero molecular motion theoretically ceases.\n- The pressure law states P is proportional to T at constant volume.\n- The general gas equation is P1V1 over T1 equals P2V2 over T2.\n- The ideal gas equation is PV equals nRT.\n- The molar gas constant R is 8.31 J per mol per kelvin in SI units.\n- Standard temperature and pressure are 273 K and 760 mmHg.\n- One mole of gas occupies 22.4 dm3 at s.t.p.\n- Dalton\'s law: total pressure equals the sum of partial pressures.\n- Gas collected over water must be corrected for water vapour pressure.\n- Graham\'s law: rate of diffusion is inversely proportional to the square root of molar mass.\n- Lighter gases diffuse faster than heavier gases.\n- Gay-Lussac\'s law of combining volumes involves simple whole-number ratios.\n- Avogadro\'s law: equal volumes of gases contain equal numbers of molecules.\n- Gas volume ratios equal mole ratios in balanced equations.\n- Kinetic theory explains pressure as molecular bombardment of container walls.\n- Heating a gas at constant volume increases pressure.\n- Compressing a gas increases the frequency of wall collisions.\n- Real gases deviate from ideality at high pressure and low temperature.\n- Deviations arise from molecular volume and intermolecular attractions.\n- Gases behave most ideally at low pressure and high temperature.\n- Always convert all quantities to consistent units before calculating.\n- Volume units may be cm3 or dm3 provided both sides match.\n- 1 dm3 equals 1000 cm3 and equals one litre.\n- Vapour density equals half the relative molecular mass.\n- Relative molecular mass equals 2 times vapour density.\n- Gas law questions frequently combine with mole and stoichiometry calculations.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
