    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Acids, Bases and Salts',
    'body': 'Acids, bases and salts form one of the most heavily examined areas of the WAEC chemistry syllabus because they link theory, calculation and practical work. According to the Arrhenius theory, an acid is a substance that produces hydrogen ions in aqueous solution, and a base produces hydroxide ions. The Bronsted-Lowry theory is broader: an acid is a proton donor and a base is a proton acceptor, so in the reaction between hydrogen chloride and water, HCl donates a proton and water accepts it, forming the hydroxonium ion and the chloride ion, which are the conjugate acid and conjugate base respectively. The Lewis theory is broader still: an acid is an electron-pair acceptor and a base is an electron-pair donor, which explains why boron trifluoride and aluminium chloride behave as acids.\n\nAcids taste sour, turn blue litmus red, have a pH below 7, react with reactive metals to liberate hydrogen, react with trioxocarbonates(IV) to give carbon(IV) oxide, and neutralise bases to form salt and water. Strong acids such as hydrochloric, trioxonitrate(V) and tetraoxosulphate(VI) acids ionise completely in water, while weak acids such as ethanoic and trioxocarbonic(IV) acids ionise only partially and exist in equilibrium with their unionised molecules. Strength must not be confused with concentration: a concentrated solution of ethanoic acid is still a weak acid, while a dilute solution of hydrochloric acid is still a strong acid.\n\nBases are oxides and hydroxides of metals; those soluble in water are called alkalis. Alkalis feel soapy, turn red litmus blue, have pH above 7, react with ammonium salts on warming to release ammonia, and neutralise acids. Sodium hydroxide and potassium hydroxide are strong alkalis, while aqueous ammonia and calcium hydroxide solution are weak. Amphoteric oxides and hydroxides such as those of aluminium, zinc and lead react with both acids and alkalis. Neutral oxides such as carbon(II) oxide, water and dinitrogen oxide react with neither.\n\nThe pH scale runs from 0 to 14 and measures the hydrogen ion concentration, with pH equal to minus the logarithm to base ten of the hydrogen ion concentration. A pH of 7 is neutral, below 7 acidic and above 7 alkaline. Indicators change colour over characteristic pH ranges: methyl orange changes from red to yellow between about 3.1 and 4.4, phenolphthalein from colourless to pink between about 8.3 and 10, and litmus in the middle range. The correct choice of indicator depends on the titration: methyl orange for strong acid against weak base, phenolphthalein for weak acid against strong base, and either for strong acid against strong alkali. No suitable indicator exists for weak acid against weak base.\n\nA salt is formed when the ionisable hydrogen of an acid is wholly or partly replaced by a metal or ammonium ion. Normal salts such as sodium chloride contain no replaceable hydrogen; acid salts such as sodium hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV) contain replaceable hydrogen; basic salts such as basic lead trioxocarbonate(IV) contain hydroxide as well; double salts such as potassium aluminium tetraoxosulphate(VI), or alum, ionise into all their component ions; and complex salts contain a complex ion. Salts are prepared by neutralisation, by the action of acid on a metal, oxide or trioxocarbonate(IV), by direct combination, and, for insoluble salts, by double decomposition or precipitation. Solubility rules should be memorised: all trioxonitrates(V) and all sodium, potassium and ammonium salts are soluble; all chlorides are soluble except those of silver and lead; all tetraoxosulphates(VI) are soluble except those of barium, calcium and lead; and all trioxocarbonates(IV) are insoluble except those of sodium, potassium and ammonium.\n\nSalts may undergo hydrolysis. A salt of a strong acid and strong base gives a neutral solution; a salt of a strong acid and weak base, such as ammonium chloride, gives an acidic solution; and a salt of a weak acid and strong base, such as sodium ethanoate, gives an alkaline solution. Deliquescent substances absorb moisture and dissolve in it, hygroscopic substances absorb moisture without dissolving, and efflorescent substances lose water of crystallisation to the air.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: Calculate the pH of a 0.01 mol per dm3 solution of hydrochloric acid.\nSolution: Hydrochloric acid is a strong monobasic acid and ionises completely, so the hydrogen ion concentration equals the acid concentration, 0.01 or 1 times 10 to the power minus 2 mol per dm3. pH equals minus log of the hydrogen ion concentration, that is minus log(10 to the minus 2). The logarithm of 10 to the minus 2 is minus 2, so the pH is 2. The solution is strongly acidic.\n\nExample 2: 25.0 cm3 of 0.1 mol per dm3 sodium hydroxide was titrated against tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid and 20.0 cm3 of the acid was required. Calculate the concentration of the acid.\nSolution: The equation is 2NaOH plus H2SO4 gives Na2SO4 plus 2H2O, so the acid to base ratio is 1 to 2. Moles of base equal 0.1 times 25.0/1000, which is 0.0025 mol, so moles of acid equal half of that, 0.00125 mol. Concentration equals 0.00125 divided by 20.0/1000, giving 0.0625 mol per dm3.\n\nExample 3: Explain why an aqueous solution of ammonium chloride turns blue litmus red.\nSolution: Ammonium chloride is the salt of a strong acid, hydrochloric acid, and a weak base, ammonia. In water the ammonium ion donates a proton to water, producing hydroxonium ions and molecular ammonia, while the chloride ion, being the conjugate base of a strong acid, does not hydrolyse. The excess hydroxonium ions make the solution acidic, so it turns blue litmus red and has a pH below 7.\n\nExample 4: Describe how Oyedele would prepare a pure dry sample of lead(II) chloride in the laboratory.\nSolution: Lead(II) chloride is insoluble, so it is prepared by double decomposition. Solutions of lead(II) trioxonitrate(V) and sodium chloride are mixed, and a white precipitate of lead(II) chloride forms immediately. The mixture is filtered, and the residue is washed with a little cold distilled water to remove soluble sodium trioxonitrate(V). The solid is then dried between filter papers or in a desiccator.\n\nExample 5: State, with reason, the indicator suitable for the titration of ethanoic acid with sodium hydroxide.\nSolution: Ethanoic acid is a weak acid and sodium hydroxide is a strong base, so the salt formed, sodium ethanoate, hydrolyses to give an alkaline solution at the equivalence point. The pH at the end point is therefore in the range of about 8 to 10. Phenolphthalein changes colour between pH 8.3 and 10 and is the correct indicator. Methyl orange would change too early and give an inaccurate titre.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- An acid produces hydrogen ions in aqueous solution.\n- A Bronsted-Lowry acid is a proton donor; a base is a proton acceptor.\n- A Lewis acid accepts an electron pair; a Lewis base donates one.\n- Conjugate acid-base pairs differ by one proton.\n- Acids turn blue litmus red and have pH below 7.\n- Acids liberate hydrogen from reactive metals.\n- Acids liberate carbon(IV) oxide from trioxocarbonates(IV).\n- Strong acids ionise completely in water.\n- Weak acids ionise only partially and exist in equilibrium.\n- Concentration and strength are different concepts.\n- Basicity of an acid is the number of replaceable hydrogen ions.\n- Hydrochloric acid is monobasic and tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid is dibasic.\n- Bases are metal oxides and hydroxides.\n- Soluble bases are called alkalis.\n- Alkalis feel soapy and turn red litmus blue.\n- Warming an alkali with an ammonium salt releases ammonia gas.\n- Amphoteric oxides react with both acids and alkalis.\n- Aluminium, zinc and lead oxides are amphoteric.\n- Carbon(II) oxide and water are neutral oxides.\n- The pH scale ranges from 0 to 14.\n- pH equals minus log of hydrogen ion concentration.\n- Methyl orange changes colour between pH 3.1 and 4.4.\n- Phenolphthalein changes colour between pH 8.3 and 10.\n- Phenolphthalein suits weak acid against strong base titrations.\n- Methyl orange suits strong acid against weak base titrations.\n- A salt forms when acid hydrogen is replaced by a metal or ammonium ion.\n- Normal salts contain no replaceable hydrogen.\n- Acid salts contain replaceable hydrogen, such as NaHCO3.\n- Double salts ionise into all their component ions in solution.\n- All trioxonitrates(V) are soluble in water.\n- All sodium, potassium and ammonium salts are soluble.\n- Silver chloride and lead(II) chloride are insoluble.\n- Barium and lead tetraoxosulphates(VI) are insoluble.\n- Insoluble salts are prepared by precipitation or double decomposition.\n- Neutralisation is the reaction of an acid with a base to give salt and water.\n- Salt hydrolysis explains why some salt solutions are not neutral.\n- Deliquescent solids absorb water and dissolve in it.\n- Efflorescent crystals lose water of crystallisation to the atmosphere.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
