    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Oxidation and Reduction Reactions',
    'body': 'Oxidation and reduction always occur together, and reactions in which they occur are called redox reactions. The concept has been extended three times. In terms of oxygen and hydrogen, oxidation is the addition of oxygen or the removal of hydrogen from a substance, while reduction is the removal of oxygen or the addition of hydrogen. In terms of electrons, oxidation is loss of electrons and reduction is gain of electrons, remembered by the mnemonic OIL RIG. In terms of oxidation number, oxidation is an increase in oxidation number and reduction is a decrease.\n\nThe oxidation number, or oxidation state, is the apparent charge an atom would carry if all bonds were ionic. The rules are: the oxidation number of a free element is zero; that of a simple ion equals its charge; hydrogen is normally plus 1 but minus 1 in metal hydrides; oxygen is normally minus 2 but minus 1 in peroxides and plus 2 in oxygen difluoride; the sum of oxidation numbers in a neutral compound is zero; and the sum in a polyatomic ion equals the charge on the ion. Applying these, the oxidation number of manganese in KMnO4 is plus 7, that of chromium in K2Cr2O7 is plus 6, that of sulphur in H2SO4 is plus 6, and that of nitrogen in HNO3 is plus 5.\n\nAn oxidising agent accepts electrons and is itself reduced; common examples are chlorine, oxygen, concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, trioxonitrate(V) acid, potassium tetraoxomanganate(VII), potassium heptaoxodichromate(VI), hydrogen peroxide and iron(III) salts. A reducing agent donates electrons and is itself oxidised; common examples are hydrogen, carbon, carbon(II) oxide, hydrogen sulphide, sulphur(IV) oxide, iron(II) salts, zinc and other reactive metals. Some substances, notably hydrogen peroxide, sulphur(IV) oxide and trioxonitrate(V) acid, can act as either an oxidising or a reducing agent depending on the conditions.\n\nTests are important for practical papers. An oxidising agent turns acidified potassium iodide solution brown by liberating iodine, and decolourises nothing; it also turns moist starch-iodide paper blue-black. A reducing agent turns acidified potassium tetraoxomanganate(VII) solution from purple to colourless and acidified potassium heptaoxodichromate(VI) from orange to green.\n\nDisproportionation is a special redox reaction in which the same element is simultaneously oxidised and reduced. When chlorine dissolves in cold dilute sodium hydroxide, chlorine at oxidation state zero is converted to chloride at minus 1 and oxochlorate(I) at plus 1. Decomposition of hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen is another example.\n\nHalf-equations are used to build full ionic equations. Each half-equation is balanced for atoms, then for oxygen using water, for hydrogen using hydrogen ions, and finally for charge using electrons. The two half-equations are then multiplied so that the electrons lost equal the electrons gained, and added together. For example, the reaction between potassium tetraoxomanganate(VII) and iron(II) ions in acid gives MnO4- plus 8H+ plus 5Fe2+ producing Mn2+ plus 4H2O plus 5Fe3+.\n\nRedox chemistry underlies extraction of metals, electrolysis, corrosion, respiration, combustion, bleaching, photography and the operation of cells and batteries. Rusting of iron, for instance, is the oxidation of iron in the presence of oxygen and water, and it is prevented by galvanising, painting, oiling, alloying or cathodic protection using a more reactive metal such as zinc or magnesium.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: Determine the oxidation number of sulphur in the tetraoxosulphate(VI) ion, SO4 2-.\nSolution: Let the oxidation number of sulphur be x. Each oxygen contributes minus 2, and there are four of them, giving minus 8. The sum of the oxidation numbers must equal the charge on the ion, which is minus 2. Therefore x plus (minus 8) equals minus 2, so x equals plus 6.\n\nExample 2: In the reaction Zn(s) plus CuSO4(aq) gives ZnSO4(aq) plus Cu(s), identify the oxidising and reducing agents.\nSolution: Zinc changes from oxidation state zero to plus 2, so it loses electrons and is oxidised, making zinc the reducing agent. Copper changes from plus 2 to zero, so it gains electrons and is reduced, making copper(II) ion the oxidising agent. The half-equations are Zn gives Zn2+ plus 2e- and Cu2+ plus 2e- gives Cu. The sulphate ion is a spectator ion.\n\nExample 3: Explain what is observed when chlorine gas is bubbled into potassium iodide solution and identify the redox changes.\nSolution: The colourless solution turns brown as iodine is liberated. Chlorine gains electrons and is reduced from zero to minus 1, so it is the oxidising agent. Iodide ions lose electrons and are oxidised from minus 1 to zero, so they act as the reducing agent. The ionic equation is Cl2 plus 2I- gives 2Cl- plus I2.\n\nExample 4: Balance the half-equation for the reduction of heptaoxodichromate(VI) ion in acidic solution.\nSolution: Start with Cr2O7 2- giving 2Cr3+. Balance oxygen by adding seven water molecules on the right, then balance hydrogen by adding fourteen hydrogen ions on the left. The left side now has a total charge of plus 12 and the right side plus 6, so six electrons are added to the left. The balanced half-equation is Cr2O7 2- plus 14H+ plus 6e- gives 2Cr3+ plus 7H2O, and the colour change is orange to green.\n\nExample 5: State two ways in which rusting of iron can be prevented and explain the chemistry of one of them.\nSolution: Rusting can be prevented by galvanising, that is coating the iron with zinc, and by cathodic protection using blocks of magnesium. In galvanising, the zinc layer excludes air and water, but more importantly zinc is higher in the electrochemical series than iron. If the coating is scratched, zinc is preferentially oxidised and acts as a sacrificial anode, so the iron does not rust. This protection continues until the zinc is consumed.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Oxidation is the addition of oxygen or removal of hydrogen.\n- Reduction is the removal of oxygen or addition of hydrogen.\n- Oxidation is loss of electrons; reduction is gain of electrons.\n- Oxidation is an increase in oxidation number.\n- Reduction is a decrease in oxidation number.\n- Oxidation and reduction always occur together in redox reactions.\n- The oxidation number of a free element is zero.\n- The oxidation number of a monatomic ion equals its charge.\n- Hydrogen is usually plus 1 but minus 1 in metal hydrides.\n- Oxygen is usually minus 2 but minus 1 in peroxides.\n- The sum of oxidation numbers in a neutral compound is zero.\n- The sum in a polyatomic ion equals the ionic charge.\n- Manganese in KMnO4 has an oxidation number of plus 7.\n- Chromium in K2Cr2O7 has an oxidation number of plus 6.\n- An oxidising agent gains electrons and is reduced.\n- A reducing agent loses electrons and is oxidised.\n- Chlorine, oxygen and trioxonitrate(V) acid are oxidising agents.\n- Hydrogen, carbon and carbon(II) oxide are reducing agents.\n- Hydrogen peroxide can act as both oxidising and reducing agent.\n- Acidified KMnO4 turns from purple to colourless with a reducing agent.\n- Acidified K2Cr2O7 turns from orange to green with a reducing agent.\n- Oxidising agents turn starch-iodide paper blue-black.\n- Disproportionation involves the same element being oxidised and reduced.\n- Chlorine in cold dilute alkali undergoes disproportionation.\n- Half-equations show electron loss or gain separately.\n- Electrons lost must equal electrons gained in the overall equation.\n- Oxygen is balanced with water and hydrogen with hydrogen ions in acid solution.\n- Combustion is a redox process involving oxidation by oxygen.\n- Respiration is a controlled redox oxidation of glucose.\n- Rusting is the oxidation of iron in the presence of air and water.\n- Rusting requires both oxygen and water.\n- Galvanising protects iron using a sacrificial coating of zinc.\n- Cathodic protection uses a more reactive metal as a sacrificial anode.\n- Metal extraction usually involves reduction of an ore.\n- Bleaching by chlorine and hydrogen peroxide is an oxidation process.\n- Electrochemical cells convert redox energy into electrical energy.',
