    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Electrolysis and Electrochemistry',
    'body': 'Electrolysis is the decomposition of an electrolyte by the passage of a direct electric current through it. An electrolyte is a substance that conducts electricity in the molten state or in aqueous solution and is decomposed in the process; examples are acids, alkalis and salts. A non-electrolyte, such as sugar solution, ethanol or benzene, does not conduct because it contains no free ions. Strong electrolytes are completely ionised and conduct well, while weak electrolytes are only partially ionised and conduct poorly. Metals and graphite conduct electricity by electron flow without decomposition and are therefore conductors rather than electrolytes.\n\nAn electrolytic cell consists of a source of direct current, an electrolyte and two electrodes. The anode is the electrode connected to the positive terminal, where oxidation occurs and anions are discharged; the cathode is connected to the negative terminal, where reduction occurs and cations are discharged. Electrodes may be inert, such as platinum or graphite, or active, such as copper in copper refining.\n\nWhen more than one ion of the same sign is present, discharge depends on three factors. The first is position in the electrochemical series: ions of less reactive metals and of less easily oxidised anions are discharged more readily. For cations, the ease of discharge increases in the order K, Na, Ca, Mg, Al, Zn, Fe, Pb, H, Cu, Hg, Ag, so copper is discharged in preference to hydrogen. For anions, the ease of discharge is generally SO4 2-, NO3-, Cl-, Br-, I-, OH-, so hydroxide is discharged in preference to sulphate. The second factor is concentration: a very concentrated chloride solution can cause chlorine to be discharged in preference to hydroxide. The third factor is the nature of the electrode: with a copper anode in copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) solution the anode itself dissolves rather than oxygen being released.\n\nElectrolysis of dilute tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid with platinum electrodes yields hydrogen at the cathode and oxygen at the anode in a volume ratio of 2 to 1, and the acid becomes more concentrated. Electrolysis of concentrated sodium chloride solution, or brine, gives hydrogen at the cathode, chlorine at the anode and sodium hydroxide in solution, the basis of the chlor-alkali industry. Electrolysis of molten sodium chloride gives sodium metal and chlorine gas. Electrolysis of copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) with copper electrodes transfers copper from the anode to the cathode without change in the concentration of the solution, and this is used to refine copper to 99.99 per cent purity.\n\nFaraday\'s first law states that the mass of a substance liberated at an electrode is directly proportional to the quantity of electricity passed, where quantity Q in coulombs equals current I in amperes multiplied by time t in seconds. Faraday\'s second law states that when the same quantity of electricity is passed through different electrolytes, the masses liberated are proportional to their chemical equivalents. One faraday, 96500 coulombs, is the quantity of electricity carried by one mole of electrons and liberates one mole of a monovalent ion, half a mole of a divalent ion and one third of a mole of a trivalent ion.\n\nApplications include electroplating for decoration and corrosion resistance, purification of metals, extraction of very reactive metals such as sodium and aluminium, anodising of aluminium and the manufacture of chlorine, hydrogen and sodium hydroxide. Electrochemical cells work in reverse: a Daniell cell converts chemical energy to electrical energy, with the more reactive metal acting as the negative electrode. The standard electrode potential measures the tendency of a half-cell to be reduced, and the electromotive force of a cell equals the electrode potential of the cathode minus that of the anode. A positive cell e.m.f. means the reaction is spontaneous.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: Calculate the mass of copper deposited when a current of 2 A is passed through copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) solution for 30 minutes. (Cu equals 64, F equals 96500 C.)\nSolution: The quantity of electricity is Q equals I times t, that is 2 times (30 times 60), which gives 3600 coulombs. The discharge equation Cu2+ plus 2e- gives Cu shows that 2 faradays, or 193000 coulombs, deposit 64 g of copper. Therefore 3600 coulombs deposit (3600 divided by 193000) times 64. This works out to about 1.19 g of copper.\n\nExample 2: State the products of the electrolysis of concentrated sodium chloride solution using inert electrodes and write the electrode equations.\nSolution: At the cathode hydrogen ions are discharged in preference to sodium ions because hydrogen is lower in the electrochemical series, giving 2H+ plus 2e- gives H2. At the anode chloride ions are discharged in preference to hydroxide ions because of their high concentration, giving 2Cl- gives Cl2 plus 2e-. Sodium and hydroxide ions remain in solution, so sodium hydroxide accumulates. The overall products are hydrogen, chlorine and sodium hydroxide.\n\nExample 3: Explain why aluminium is extracted by electrolysis rather than by reduction with carbon.\nSolution: Aluminium is a very reactive metal high in the electrochemical series, so its oxide is very stable and cannot be reduced by carbon at economically attainable temperatures. Electrolysis of purified alumina dissolved in molten cryolite supplies the strong reducing power needed. Cryolite lowers the melting point from over 2000 degrees Celsius to about 950 degrees Celsius, saving energy. Aluminium is deposited at the carbon cathode while oxygen forms at the carbon anodes, which burn away and must be replaced.\n\nExample 4: The same quantity of electricity is passed through solutions of silver trioxonitrate(V) and copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) in series. If 10.8 g of silver is deposited, what mass of copper is deposited? (Ag equals 108, Cu equals 64.)\nSolution: Moles of silver deposited equal 10.8 divided by 108, which is 0.1 mol, and since Ag+ plus e- gives Ag, this requires 0.1 faraday. Copper requires two electrons per atom, so 0.1 faraday deposits 0.05 mol of copper. Mass equals 0.05 times 64, which gives 3.2 g of copper.\n\nExample 5: Describe how Toluwani would electroplate an iron spoon with silver, stating the electrodes and electrolyte.\nSolution: The iron spoon is made the cathode so that silver ions are reduced and deposited on it. A pure silver plate is used as the anode so that it dissolves and replenishes the solution. The electrolyte is a solution of a soluble silver salt, usually silver cyanide complex or silver trioxonitrate(V). A small, steady current is used for a long time so that the deposit is smooth and adherent, and the spoon must first be cleaned thoroughly to remove grease and oxide.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Electrolysis is decomposition of an electrolyte by direct electric current.\n- Electrolytes conduct electricity when molten or in aqueous solution.\n- Non-electrolytes contain no free ions and do not conduct.\n- Strong electrolytes are completely ionised in solution.\n- Weak electrolytes are only partially ionised.\n- Metals conduct by electron flow without decomposition.\n- The anode is positive and is the site of oxidation.\n- The cathode is negative and is the site of reduction.\n- Cations migrate to the cathode and anions to the anode.\n- Discharge depends on position in the electrochemical series.\n- Discharge also depends on ion concentration.\n- Discharge also depends on the nature of the electrode.\n- Inert electrodes include platinum and graphite.\n- Copper anodes dissolve during electrolysis of copper salts.\n- Electrolysis of acidified water gives hydrogen and oxygen in a 2 to 1 ratio.\n- Electrolysis of brine gives hydrogen, chlorine and sodium hydroxide.\n- Electrolysis of molten sodium chloride gives sodium and chlorine.\n- Faraday\'s first law relates mass liberated to quantity of electricity.\n- Quantity of electricity Q equals current times time.\n- One faraday equals 96500 coulombs.\n- One faraday equals the charge on one mole of electrons.\n- One faraday liberates one mole of a monovalent ion.\n- Two faradays are needed for one mole of a divalent ion.\n- Faraday\'s second law relates masses to chemical equivalents.\n- Chemical equivalent equals molar mass divided by valency.\n- Electroplating uses the object as the cathode.\n- Electroplating improves appearance and prevents corrosion.\n- Copper is refined by electrolysis with impure copper as anode.\n- Anode mud from copper refining contains silver and gold.\n- Aluminium is extracted by electrolysis of alumina in molten cryolite.\n- Cryolite lowers the melting point and saves energy.\n- Electrochemical cells convert chemical energy into electrical energy.\n- In a cell the more reactive metal is the negative electrode.\n- Cell e.m.f. equals cathode potential minus anode potential.\n- A positive e.m.f. indicates a spontaneous cell reaction.\n- The electrochemical series predicts displacement and discharge behaviour.',
