    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Rate of Chemical Reaction',
    'body': 'The rate of a chemical reaction is the change in concentration of a reactant or product per unit time. It can also be expressed as the amount of reactant used up per unit time or the amount of product formed per unit time, with units such as mol per dm3 per second, cm3 of gas per second, or grams per minute. Reactions vary enormously in rate: precipitation and neutralisation are almost instantaneous, while rusting and fermentation are slow.\n\nThe collision theory explains rates. For a reaction to occur, reacting particles must collide, the collision must have energy equal to or greater than the activation energy, and the particles must be correctly oriented. Activation energy is the minimum energy that colliding particles must possess for a reaction to occur, and it represents the energy barrier over which reactants must pass to become products. Anything that increases either the frequency of collisions or the proportion of collisions that are effective will increase the rate.\n\nConcentration is the first factor. Increasing the concentration of a reactant in solution increases the number of particles per unit volume, so collisions become more frequent and the rate rises. For gases, increasing pressure has the same effect as increasing concentration, because the same number of molecules is squeezed into a smaller volume.\n\nTemperature is the second and most dramatic factor. Raising the temperature increases the average kinetic energy of particles, so they move faster and collide more often, but more importantly a much larger fraction of collisions now exceeds the activation energy. As a rough rule, a rise of 10 degrees Celsius roughly doubles the rate of many reactions.\n\nSurface area of a solid reactant is the third factor. Powdered calcium trioxocarbonate(IV) reacts far faster with acid than a single lump of the same mass because a greater surface area exposes more particles to collision. This is why dust explosions occur in flour mills and why finely divided catalysts are used in industry.\n\nCatalysts form the fourth factor. A catalyst is a substance that alters the rate of a chemical reaction while remaining chemically unchanged in mass and composition at the end. A positive catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway of lower activation energy, so more collisions are effective. An inhibitor or negative catalyst slows a reaction. Catalysts are specific, are needed only in small amounts, do not change the position of equilibrium, and can be poisoned by impurities. Examples include manganese(IV) oxide in the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide, iron in the Haber process, vanadium(V) oxide in the contact process and enzymes in biological systems.\n\nLight is a fifth factor for photochemical reactions such as photosynthesis, the reaction of methane with chlorine and the decomposition of silver halides in photography. The nature of the reactants also matters: ionic reactions in solution are usually fast because no bonds need to be broken, whereas covalent reactions are slower.\n\nRates are measured experimentally by following the volume of gas evolved with time, the loss in mass of a reaction mixture, the time taken for a precipitate to obscure a mark under a flask, the change in colour or in pH, or by titrating samples withdrawn at intervals. A graph of product formed against time is steepest at the start, when concentrations are highest, and levels off as reactants are used up; the slope of the tangent at any point gives the instantaneous rate.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: 50 cm3 of hydrogen was collected in 25 seconds in a reaction between zinc and dilute acid. Calculate the average rate of reaction.\nSolution: Average rate equals the volume of gas produced divided by the time taken. Substituting, the rate equals 50 cm3 divided by 25 s. This gives 2 cm3 per second. The rate would be highest at the start of the reaction and would fall as the acid is used up.\n\nExample 2: Explain, using collision theory, why powdered marble reacts faster with dilute hydrochloric acid than the same mass of marble chips.\nSolution: The powder has a much larger total surface area than the chips of the same mass, so far more carbonate particles are exposed to the acid at any moment. This increases the frequency of collisions between hydrogen ions and the solid surface. Since the frequency of effective collisions rises, the rate of production of carbon(IV) oxide increases. The total volume of gas produced is unchanged because the amount of marble is the same.\n\nExample 3: Explain why a rise of 10 degrees Celsius can double the rate of a reaction even though the average kinetic energy rises only slightly.\nSolution: Rate depends not on the average energy but on the fraction of molecules with energy exceeding the activation energy. The Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution shows that this fraction lies in the high-energy tail of the curve. A small rise in temperature shifts the whole curve to the right and greatly increases the area under the tail beyond the activation energy. The number of effective collisions therefore increases far more than the collision frequency alone.\n\nExample 4: Describe an experiment to investigate the effect of concentration on the rate of the reaction between sodium thiosulphate and hydrochloric acid.\nSolution: A fixed volume of sodium thiosulphate solution is placed in a conical flask standing over a paper marked with a cross. A fixed volume of dilute hydrochloric acid is added and a stopwatch started immediately. The time taken for the sulphur precipitate to obscure the cross is recorded, and the experiment is repeated with the thiosulphate diluted to different known concentrations while keeping the total volume constant. A graph of 1 over time, which is proportional to rate, against concentration gives a straight line, showing that rate increases with concentration.\n\nExample 5: Manganese(IV) oxide is added to hydrogen peroxide solution. State the observation and explain the role of the solid.\nSolution: Effervescence occurs immediately as oxygen gas is rapidly evolved, and the gas relights a glowing splint. Manganese(IV) oxide acts as a catalyst, providing an alternative reaction path with lower activation energy for the decomposition 2H2O2 gives 2H2O plus O2. The solid can be filtered off, dried and weighed at the end and its mass is unchanged, confirming that it is not consumed.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Rate of reaction is the change in concentration per unit time.\n- Rate may be measured as reactant used or product formed per unit time.\n- Collision theory requires particles to collide to react.\n- Colliding particles must have energy equal to or above activation energy.\n- Colliding particles must also have the correct orientation.\n- Activation energy is the minimum energy needed for reaction.\n- Increasing concentration increases collision frequency and rate.\n- Increasing gas pressure increases concentration and rate.\n- Increasing temperature increases the fraction of effective collisions.\n- A rise of 10 degrees Celsius roughly doubles many reaction rates.\n- Increasing surface area of a solid increases rate.\n- Powders react faster than lumps of the same mass.\n- Dust explosions illustrate the danger of large surface areas.\n- A catalyst alters rate but is chemically unchanged at the end.\n- A catalyst provides an alternative path of lower activation energy.\n- A catalyst does not change the position of equilibrium.\n- A catalyst does not change the amount of product formed.\n- Catalysts are specific and needed only in small amounts.\n- Catalysts can be poisoned by impurities.\n- Manganese(IV) oxide catalyses decomposition of hydrogen peroxide.\n- Iron is the catalyst in the Haber process.\n- Vanadium(V) oxide is the catalyst in the contact process.\n- Enzymes are biological catalysts that work best at optimum pH and temperature.\n- Light affects the rate of photochemical reactions.\n- Photosynthesis and photography are light-dependent reactions.\n- Ionic reactions in solution are usually very fast.\n- Reactions involving breaking covalent bonds are usually slower.\n- Rate is highest at the beginning of a reaction.\n- Rate decreases as reactants are consumed.\n- A rate graph levels off when a reactant is exhausted.\n- The slope of the tangent to a rate curve gives the instantaneous rate.\n- Gas evolution can be measured with a gas syringe or over water.\n- Loss in mass can be followed on a balance for reactions producing gas.\n- The thiosulphate and acid reaction is followed by disappearance of a cross.\n- Changing concentration does not change the total yield of product.\n- Understanding rates is essential for industrial process design.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
