    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Chemical Equilibrium',
    'body': 'Many chemical reactions do not go to completion. A reversible reaction is one that can proceed in both the forward and backward directions under the same conditions, and it is written with a double arrow. When such a reaction takes place in a closed system, it eventually reaches a state of dynamic equilibrium in which the rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the backward reaction, so the concentrations of reactants and products remain constant although both reactions continue at the molecular level.\n\nThe characteristics of dynamic equilibrium are important for WAEC answers. Equilibrium is attained only in a closed system; both reactions continue, so the equilibrium is dynamic and not static; the observable properties such as colour, pressure and concentration remain constant with time; equilibrium can be approached from either direction; and a catalyst hastens the attainment of equilibrium without altering its position.\n\nFor a general reaction in which aA plus bB gives cC plus dD, the equilibrium constant in terms of concentration is Kc, equal to the product of the concentrations of the products, each raised to the power of its coefficient, divided by the corresponding product for the reactants. A large value of Kc indicates that the position of equilibrium lies far to the right and the yield of products is high, while a small value indicates that reactants predominate. Kc changes only with temperature; it is unaffected by pressure, concentration or the presence of a catalyst. For gaseous reactions the constant may also be written in terms of partial pressures as Kp.\n\nLe Chatelier\'s principle states that when a constraint is applied to a system in equilibrium, the system adjusts itself so as to nullify or minimise the effect of the constraint. Increasing the concentration of a reactant shifts the equilibrium to the right, favouring more products, while removing a product also shifts it to the right. Increasing pressure shifts the equilibrium in the direction that produces fewer moles of gas; if the number of gaseous moles is the same on both sides, pressure has no effect. Increasing temperature favours the endothermic direction, while decreasing temperature favours the exothermic direction. A catalyst has no effect on the position of equilibrium, only on the time taken to reach it.\n\nThese ideas are applied directly in industry. In the Haber process for ammonia, nitrogen and hydrogen combine in an exothermic reaction with a decrease in the number of gaseous moles from four to two. High pressure of about 200 atmospheres therefore increases yield, and low temperature would also increase yield, but at low temperature the rate would be uneconomically slow. A compromise temperature of about 450 to 500 degrees Celsius is used with an iron catalyst, and ammonia is removed by liquefaction so that the equilibrium keeps shifting to the right. In the contact process for tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, sulphur(IV) oxide and oxygen combine exothermically with a decrease in gaseous moles, so moderate pressure, a temperature of about 450 degrees Celsius and vanadium(V) oxide catalyst are used.\n\nOther equilibria include the ionisation of weak acids and bases, the solubility equilibrium of a sparingly soluble salt described by its solubility product, and the common ion effect, in which the addition of an ion already present suppresses ionisation or reduces solubility. Buffer solutions resist changes in pH and consist of a weak acid with its salt, such as ethanoic acid and sodium ethanoate, or a weak base with its salt.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: For the reaction N2(g) plus 3H2(g) giving 2NH3(g), state the effect of increasing pressure and explain.\nSolution: There are four moles of gas on the left and two moles on the right. By Le Chatelier\'s principle, increasing pressure shifts the equilibrium in the direction that reduces the number of gaseous molecules, that is to the right. The yield of ammonia therefore increases. This is why the Haber process is operated at high pressure.\n\nExample 2: Write the expression for the equilibrium constant Kc for the reaction 2SO2(g) plus O2(g) giving 2SO3(g).\nSolution: Kc equals the concentration of SO3 squared, divided by the product of the concentration of SO2 squared and the concentration of O2. Each concentration is raised to the power of its stoichiometric coefficient. The units would be dm3 per mol, obtained from the difference in powers. Kc for this reaction has a large value at moderate temperature, showing a high yield of sulphur(VI) oxide.\n\nExample 3: The forward reaction in a certain equilibrium is exothermic. Predict and explain the effect of raising the temperature on the yield of the product and on the value of Kc.\nSolution: Raising the temperature adds heat to the system, so by Le Chatelier\'s principle the equilibrium shifts in the endothermic direction to absorb the added heat. Since the forward reaction is exothermic, the backward reaction is endothermic, so the equilibrium shifts to the left. The yield of product therefore decreases, and the value of Kc becomes smaller. Temperature is the only factor that changes the numerical value of Kc.\n\nExample 4: Explain why a catalyst is still used in the Haber process even though it does not increase the yield of ammonia.\nSolution: The equilibrium yield depends only on temperature, pressure and concentrations, so an iron catalyst leaves it unchanged. However, the reaction between nitrogen and hydrogen has a very high activation energy because the nitrogen triple bond is strong, and without a catalyst the equilibrium would be reached far too slowly. The catalyst lowers the activation energy and allows equilibrium to be attained quickly at a moderate temperature. This makes the process economically viable for a plant such as one operated by Zetra.\n\nExample 5: Explain the common ion effect using the ionisation of ethanoic acid on addition of sodium ethanoate.\nSolution: Ethanoic acid ionises only partially, giving an equilibrium between the molecular acid and its hydrogen and ethanoate ions. Adding sodium ethanoate greatly increases the concentration of ethanoate ions in the solution. By Le Chatelier\'s principle the equilibrium shifts to the left to reduce this increase, so the degree of ionisation of the acid falls and the hydrogen ion concentration decreases. The pH therefore rises, and the resulting mixture acts as an acidic buffer.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- A reversible reaction proceeds in both directions under the same conditions.\n- Reversible reactions are written with a double arrow.\n- Equilibrium is reached only in a closed system.\n- At equilibrium the forward and backward rates are equal.\n- Equilibrium is dynamic, not static.\n- Observable properties remain constant at equilibrium.\n- Equilibrium can be approached from either direction.\n- Kc is the equilibrium constant in terms of concentration.\n- Kp is the equilibrium constant in terms of partial pressure.\n- Products appear in the numerator of the Kc expression.\n- Coefficients in the equation become powers in the expression.\n- A large Kc means the equilibrium lies to the right.\n- A small Kc means reactants predominate.\n- Kc changes only with temperature.\n- Catalysts do not change Kc or the position of equilibrium.\n- Le Chatelier\'s principle predicts the response to a constraint.\n- Increasing reactant concentration shifts equilibrium to the right.\n- Removing a product shifts equilibrium to the right.\n- Increasing pressure favours the side with fewer gaseous moles.\n- Pressure has no effect when moles of gas are equal on both sides.\n- Increasing temperature favours the endothermic direction.\n- Decreasing temperature favours the exothermic direction.\n- The Haber process uses about 200 atmospheres and 450 degrees Celsius.\n- Iron is the catalyst in the Haber process.\n- Ammonia is removed by liquefaction to shift equilibrium right.\n- The contact process converts sulphur(IV) oxide to sulphur(VI) oxide.\n- Vanadium(V) oxide catalyses the contact process.\n- Industrial conditions are compromises between yield and rate.\n- Weak acids and bases exist in ionic equilibrium in solution.\n- The common ion effect suppresses the ionisation of a weak electrolyte.\n- The common ion effect reduces the solubility of a sparingly soluble salt.\n- Solubility product applies to saturated solutions of sparingly soluble salts.\n- Precipitation occurs when the ionic product exceeds the solubility product.\n- A buffer solution resists change in pH on adding small amounts of acid or base.\n- An acidic buffer contains a weak acid and its salt.\n- Blood and many industrial processes depend on buffering.',
