    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Energy Changes in Chemical Reactions',
    'body': 'Every chemical reaction involves an energy change, because energy is required to break bonds in the reactants and energy is released when new bonds form in the products. Thermochemistry is the study of these heat changes. The system is the reacting substances, and the surroundings are everything else. Enthalpy, H, is the heat content of a system at constant pressure, and the enthalpy change, delta H, equals the enthalpy of the products minus the enthalpy of the reactants.\n\nAn exothermic reaction releases heat to the surroundings, so the products have less energy than the reactants and delta H is negative. Examples include combustion, neutralisation, respiration, rusting, most displacement reactions and the dissolution of concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid or sodium hydroxide in water. The temperature of the reaction mixture rises. An endothermic reaction absorbs heat from the surroundings, so the products have more energy than the reactants and delta H is positive. Examples include photosynthesis, thermal decomposition of trioxocarbonates(IV), the reaction of carbon with steam, and the dissolution of ammonium trioxonitrate(V) or potassium trioxonitrate(V) in water, which causes a temperature drop.\n\nEnergy profile diagrams show the path from reactants to products. In an exothermic profile the products lie below the reactants, while in an endothermic profile they lie above. In both cases the curve rises to a peak that represents the activation energy, the energy of the transition state or activated complex. The size of the activation energy governs the rate, while the value of delta H governs whether heat is released or absorbed; the two are independent.\n\nSeveral standard enthalpy changes must be defined. The heat of reaction is the heat change when molar quantities react as shown in the equation. The heat of formation is the heat change when one mole of a compound is formed from its elements in their standard states. The heat of combustion is the heat change when one mole of a substance is completely burnt in oxygen. The heat of neutralisation is the heat change when one mole of hydrogen ions from an acid reacts with one mole of hydroxide ions from an alkali to form one mole of water; for strong acid and strong alkali this is almost constant at about minus 57.3 kJ per mole because the reaction is simply H+ plus OH- giving H2O. The heat of solution is the heat change when one mole of a substance dissolves in a large excess of water, and the heat of displacement, heat of atomisation and lattice energy are similarly defined.\n\nHess\'s law states that the total enthalpy change in a chemical reaction is independent of the route taken, provided the initial and final conditions are the same. It is a consequence of the law of conservation of energy and allows enthalpy changes that cannot be measured directly, such as the heat of formation of carbon(II) oxide, to be calculated from those that can. Energy cycles such as the Born-Haber cycle apply the same reasoning to the formation of ionic compounds from their elements through atomisation, ionisation, electron affinity and lattice energy.\n\nEntropy, S, is a measure of disorder, and it increases when a solid melts, a liquid vaporises, a solid dissolves or the number of gaseous molecules increases. A reaction is spontaneous when the free energy change, delta G, equal to delta H minus T delta S, is negative. An exothermic reaction with an increase in entropy is spontaneous at all temperatures, while an endothermic reaction with increased entropy becomes spontaneous only at high temperature.\n\nThe practical measurement of heat change uses the relation heat equals mass multiplied by specific heat capacity multiplied by temperature change, and the molar enthalpy is obtained by dividing this heat by the number of moles that reacted.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: When 50 cm3 of 1 mol per dm3 hydrochloric acid was mixed with 50 cm3 of 1 mol per dm3 sodium hydroxide, the temperature rose by 6.8 degrees Celsius. Calculate the heat of neutralisation. (Specific heat capacity equals 4.2 J per g per K; density equals 1 g per cm3.)\nSolution: The total volume is 100 cm3, so the mass of solution is 100 g. Heat evolved equals mass times specific heat capacity times temperature change, that is 100 times 4.2 times 6.8, which equals 2856 J or 2.856 kJ. Moles of water formed equal 1 times 50/1000, that is 0.05 mol. The heat of neutralisation is therefore minus 2.856 divided by 0.05, which is about minus 57.1 kJ per mole.\n\nExample 2: Given that the heat of combustion of carbon is minus 393 kJ per mole and that of carbon(II) oxide is minus 283 kJ per mole, use Hess\'s law to find the heat of formation of carbon(II) oxide.\nSolution: Carbon can be burnt directly to carbon(IV) oxide, releasing 393 kJ, or it can first form carbon(II) oxide and then burn to carbon(IV) oxide, releasing 283 kJ in the second step. By Hess\'s law the total for the two-step route equals the one-step value, so delta Hf of CO plus (minus 283) equals minus 393. Therefore delta Hf of CO equals minus 393 plus 283, which is minus 110 kJ per mole.\n\nExample 3: Explain why the heat of neutralisation of ethanoic acid with sodium hydroxide is numerically less than 57.3 kJ per mole.\nSolution: For strong acid and strong alkali the only reaction is between free hydrogen and hydroxide ions, giving a constant value. Ethanoic acid is a weak acid and is only partially ionised in solution. Some of the heat released by neutralisation is absorbed in ionising the remaining acid molecules. The net heat evolved is therefore smaller, typically about 55 kJ per mole.\n\nExample 4: A reaction has delta H equal to plus 40 kJ per mole and a positive entropy change. Discuss its spontaneity.\nSolution: Free energy change is given by delta G equals delta H minus T delta S. Since delta H is positive, the first term opposes spontaneity, but because delta S is positive, the term minus T delta S is negative and becomes more negative as temperature rises. At low temperature delta G is positive and the reaction is not spontaneous, but above a certain temperature the entropy term dominates and delta G becomes negative. The reaction is therefore spontaneous only at sufficiently high temperature, which is typical of thermal decompositions.\n\nExample 5: Distinguish clearly between activation energy and enthalpy change using an energy profile.\nSolution: The activation energy is the height of the energy barrier from the reactants to the peak of the curve, and it determines how fast the reaction occurs. The enthalpy change is the vertical difference between the energy level of the reactants and that of the products, and it determines whether heat is absorbed or released. A reaction can be strongly exothermic yet very slow if the activation energy is high, as with the combustion of petrol at room temperature. Adding a catalyst lowers the activation energy but leaves delta H unchanged.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Bond breaking absorbs energy and is endothermic.\n- Bond making releases energy and is exothermic.\n- Enthalpy change equals enthalpy of products minus that of reactants.\n- Exothermic reactions have negative delta H and release heat.\n- Endothermic reactions have positive delta H and absorb heat.\n- Combustion and neutralisation are exothermic.\n- Photosynthesis and thermal decomposition are endothermic.\n- Dissolving ammonium trioxonitrate(V) in water lowers the temperature.\n- Dissolving concentrated acid or sodium hydroxide in water raises the temperature.\n- Energy profile diagrams show reactant, transition state and product levels.\n- Activation energy is the barrier between reactants and products.\n- A catalyst lowers activation energy but not delta H.\n- Heat of reaction refers to molar amounts in the equation.\n- Heat of formation forms one mole of compound from its elements.\n- Heat of combustion burns one mole of substance completely in oxygen.\n- Heat of neutralisation forms one mole of water from acid and alkali.\n- Heat of neutralisation of strong acid and strong alkali is about minus 57.3 kJ per mole.\n- Weak acids give smaller heats of neutralisation due to ionisation energy.\n- Heat of solution is measured for one mole dissolved in excess water.\n- Lattice energy relates to the formation of an ionic lattice from gaseous ions.\n- Hess\'s law states that enthalpy change is independent of route.\n- Hess\'s law follows from the law of conservation of energy.\n- Hess\'s law allows calculation of immeasurable enthalpy changes.\n- Born-Haber cycles apply Hess\'s law to ionic compounds.\n- Standard conditions are 298 K and 1 atmosphere.\n- The enthalpy of formation of an element in its standard state is zero.\n- Heat change equals mass times specific heat capacity times temperature change.\n- Specific heat capacity of water is 4.2 J per g per K.\n- Molar enthalpy is heat change divided by moles reacted.\n- Entropy is a measure of disorder in a system.\n- Entropy increases on melting, vaporising and dissolving.\n- Free energy change equals delta H minus T delta S.\n- A negative delta G indicates a spontaneous reaction.\n- Exothermic reactions with increased entropy are always spontaneous.\n- Endothermic reactions with increased entropy are spontaneous at high temperature.\n- Fuel value comparisons rely on heats of combustion per gram.',
