    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Solubility and Solutions',
    'body': 'A solution is a homogeneous mixture of two or more substances. The solute is the substance that dissolves, and the solvent is the substance in which it dissolves; in a sugar solution, sugar is the solute and water the solvent. Water is called the universal solvent because it dissolves a very wide range of ionic and polar substances, while non-polar substances such as fats, oils and rubber dissolve in non-polar solvents such as benzene, kerosene and tetrachloromethane. The general rule is that like dissolves like: polar solvents dissolve polar and ionic solutes, and non-polar solvents dissolve non-polar solutes.\n\nA solution may be unsaturated, saturated or supersaturated. An unsaturated solution can still dissolve more solute at that temperature. A saturated solution is one that contains as much solute as it can dissolve at a particular temperature in the presence of undissolved solute. A supersaturated solution contains more solute than a saturated solution at that temperature and is unstable; adding a tiny seed crystal or scratching the container causes immediate crystallisation.\n\nSolubility is defined as the maximum amount of a solute, in moles or grams, that will dissolve in one cubic decimetre, or in 100 g, of solvent at a particular temperature to form a saturated solution. Solubility must always be quoted with the temperature. It is determined experimentally by evaporating a known volume or mass of saturated solution to dryness and weighing the residue, and then scaling up to 100 g of solvent using the relation solubility equals mass of solute divided by mass of solvent multiplied by 100.\n\nA solubility curve is a graph of solubility against temperature. For most solids, such as potassium trioxonitrate(V), solubility increases sharply with temperature because dissolution is endothermic. For a few salts, such as calcium ethanoate and calcium tetraoxosulphate(VI), solubility decreases with rising temperature. Sodium chloride has a curve that is almost flat. Solubility curves are used to determine the solubility at any temperature, to find the mass of crystals deposited when a saturated solution is cooled, and to select conditions for purification by fractional crystallisation, which separates two salts whose solubilities change differently with temperature.\n\nThe solubility of gases in liquids behaves oppositely: it decreases with increase in temperature, which is why boiled water tastes flat and why warm river water holds less dissolved oxygen for fish, and it increases with increase in pressure, as described by Henry\'s law and demonstrated by the fizz released when a bottle of soft drink is opened.\n\nDissolution involves an energy change. Lattice energy must be supplied to separate the ions, while hydration energy is released when the ions are surrounded by water molecules. If the hydration energy exceeds the lattice energy the process is exothermic and the solution warms, as with sodium hydroxide; if the lattice energy is greater, the process is endothermic and the solution cools, as with ammonium trioxonitrate(V).\n\nRelated concepts include crystallisation, in which crystals with water of crystallisation, called hydrated salts, are deposited on cooling; efflorescence, deliquescence and hygroscopy; and the effect of a solute on the physical properties of the solvent, since dissolving a non-volatile solute lowers the freezing point and raises the boiling point of the solvent. Separation techniques such as evaporation, crystallisation, fractional crystallisation, filtration, precipitation and solvent extraction all rest on solubility differences.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: 25 cm3 of a saturated solution of a salt gave 5.0 g of dry residue on evaporation at 30 degrees Celsius. Calculate the solubility in g per dm3 and in g per 100 g of water (density of solution assumed 1 g per cm3).\nSolution: Since 25 cm3 gave 5.0 g, one cubic decimetre, that is 1000 cm3, would give 5.0 times 1000/25, which equals 200 g. The solubility is therefore 200 g per dm3 at 30 degrees Celsius. Expressed per 100 g of water this is approximately 20 g per 100 g, assuming the mass of water is close to the mass of solution.\n\nExample 2: The solubility of potassium trioxonitrate(V) is 110 g per 100 g of water at 60 degrees Celsius and 32 g per 100 g at 20 degrees Celsius. What mass of crystals is deposited when 250 g of saturated solution at 60 degrees Celsius is cooled to 20 degrees Celsius?\nSolution: At 60 degrees Celsius, 210 g of saturated solution contains 110 g of salt and 100 g of water, so 250 g of solution contains 100 times 250/210, that is about 119 g of water, and 250 minus 119, that is about 131 g of salt. On cooling to 20 degrees Celsius, that 119 g of water can hold only 32 times 119/100, which is about 38 g of salt. The mass of crystals deposited is therefore about 131 minus 38, that is roughly 93 g.\n\nExample 3: Explain why a soft drink fizzes when the bottle is opened.\nSolution: The drink is bottled with carbon(IV) oxide dissolved under high pressure, and the solubility of a gas increases with pressure. When the bottle is opened the pressure above the liquid falls suddenly to atmospheric pressure. The solubility of the gas therefore falls sharply, and the excess dissolved gas escapes rapidly as bubbles. Warming the drink reduces gas solubility further and increases the fizzing.\n\nExample 4: Distinguish between a saturated and a supersaturated solution and describe how a supersaturated solution can be made.\nSolution: A saturated solution contains the maximum solute it can hold at a given temperature in contact with undissolved solute, whereas a supersaturated solution contains more solute than that maximum and is therefore unstable. A supersaturated solution is prepared by making a saturated solution at a high temperature and then cooling it slowly and undisturbed so that crystallisation does not begin. Adding a seed crystal, stirring or scratching the vessel wall causes rapid crystallisation and the release of heat, restoring the ordinary saturated state.\n\nExample 5: Describe how Oyedele could separate a mixture of sodium chloride and potassium trioxonitrate(V) using their solubility curves.\nSolution: The solubility of potassium trioxonitrate(V) rises steeply with temperature while that of sodium chloride is almost constant. The mixture is dissolved in the minimum quantity of hot water so that both salts are in solution. On cooling, the solution becomes supersaturated with respect to potassium trioxonitrate(V) only, so pure crystals of that salt separate out and are filtered off. The sodium chloride remains largely in the filtrate, and the process is called fractional crystallisation.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- A solution is a homogeneous mixture of solute and solvent.\n- The solute is the dissolved substance; the solvent does the dissolving.\n- Water is described as the universal solvent.\n- Like dissolves like: polar solvents dissolve polar and ionic solutes.\n- Non-polar solutes dissolve in non-polar solvents such as benzene.\n- An unsaturated solution can dissolve more solute at that temperature.\n- A saturated solution holds the maximum solute in contact with undissolved solid.\n- A supersaturated solution holds more solute than saturation allows.\n- Supersaturated solutions crystallise on seeding or scratching.\n- Solubility is quoted in moles or grams per dm3 or per 100 g of solvent.\n- Solubility must always be stated at a specific temperature.\n- Solubility is determined by evaporating a saturated solution to dryness.\n- A solubility curve plots solubility against temperature.\n- Most solids become more soluble as temperature rises.\n- Calcium tetraoxosulphate(VI) becomes less soluble as temperature rises.\n- Sodium chloride solubility changes very little with temperature.\n- Solubility curves are used to find crystals deposited on cooling.\n- Fractional crystallisation separates salts with different solubility curves.\n- Gas solubility decreases with increasing temperature.\n- Gas solubility increases with increasing pressure.\n- Warm water holds less dissolved oxygen, affecting aquatic life.\n- Dissolution involves lattice energy and hydration energy.\n- Dissolution is exothermic when hydration energy exceeds lattice energy.\n- Dissolution is endothermic when lattice energy exceeds hydration energy.\n- Hydrated salts contain water of crystallisation.\n- Efflorescent crystals lose water of crystallisation to the air.\n- Deliquescent substances absorb moisture and dissolve in it.\n- Hygroscopic substances absorb moisture without dissolving.\n- Dissolving a non-volatile solute raises the boiling point of a solvent.\n- Dissolving a non-volatile solute lowers the freezing point of a solvent.\n- Filtration separates an insoluble solid from a liquid.\n- Evaporation recovers a soluble solid that is stable to heat.\n- Crystallisation recovers salts that decompose on strong heating.\n- Precipitation forms an insoluble salt from two soluble ones.\n- Solvent extraction separates a solute using two immiscible solvents.\n- Chromatography separates dissolved substances by differing solubility and adsorption.',
