    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Water and Hardness of Water',
    'body': 'Water is the most abundant and most important compound on earth. It is a covalent compound with the formula H2O, in which the oxygen atom is joined to two hydrogen atoms by single covalent bonds with a bond angle of about 104.5 degrees, giving a bent or V-shaped molecule. Because oxygen is far more electronegative than hydrogen, the molecule is polar and forms extensive hydrogen bonds. These hydrogen bonds explain water\'s unusually high boiling point of 100 degrees Celsius, its high specific heat capacity and latent heat, its high surface tension, its excellent solvent power for ionic and polar substances, and the fact that ice is less dense than liquid water, so that ice floats and aquatic life survives in cold climates. Water has a maximum density at 4 degrees Celsius.\n\nChemically, pure water is neutral to litmus, has a pH of 7, and is very slightly ionised into hydrogen and hydroxide ions. Water reacts with reactive metals such as sodium and potassium to give hydrogen and an alkaline hydroxide, and with less reactive metals such as magnesium, zinc and iron only as steam, giving the metal oxide and hydrogen. It reacts with metallic oxides of reactive metals to give alkalis, and with non-metallic oxides such as sulphur(IV) oxide and carbon(IV) oxide to give acids. It hydrolyses certain salts and reacts with anhydrous salts to form hydrates; anhydrous copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) turns from white to blue and anhydrous cobalt(II) chloride from blue to pink, and both are used as tests for water. Only the restoration of a boiling point of exactly 100 degrees Celsius and a freezing point of exactly 0 degrees Celsius at standard pressure proves that a sample is pure water.\n\nWater is treated for town supply by sedimentation to allow suspended matter to settle, coagulation with alum, filtration through sand and gravel beds, chlorination or ozonation to kill bacteria, and sometimes aeration to improve taste and fluoridation to protect teeth.\n\nHardness of water is the property of water that prevents it from readily forming lather with soap, producing instead an insoluble scum. It is caused by dissolved salts of calcium and magnesium. Temporary hardness is caused by the hydrogentrioxocarbonates(IV) of calcium and magnesium, formed when rainwater containing dissolved carbon(IV) oxide passes over limestone. It is removed simply by boiling, which decomposes the hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV) into insoluble trioxocarbonate(IV), water and carbon(IV) oxide, and also by adding calcium hydroxide in calculated amount, a method called Clark\'s method. Permanent hardness is caused by the tetraoxosulphates(VI) and chlorides of calcium and magnesium, and it is not removed by boiling. It is removed by adding washing soda, sodium trioxocarbonate(IV), which precipitates the calcium and magnesium as trioxocarbonates(IV), by the permutit or ion-exchange method in which sodium ions replace calcium and magnesium ions, by using synthetic resins that remove all ions to give deionised water, or by distillation.\n\nHardness has both disadvantages and advantages. It wastes soap, forms scum that stains fabrics, causes furring or scale in kettles, boilers and pipes which wastes fuel and can cause boiler explosions, and blocks pipes. However, hard water supplies calcium needed for bones and teeth, tastes pleasant, is valuable in brewing and tanning, and reduces lead poisoning by coating lead pipes with an insoluble layer.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: Explain why ice floats on water and state one biological importance of this.\nSolution: When water freezes, each molecule forms hydrogen bonds with four neighbours in a rigid, open, tetrahedral lattice containing large empty spaces. This structure occupies a greater volume than the same mass of liquid water, in which the molecules are more closely packed. Ice is therefore less dense than water and floats. Because the ice layer floats on top and insulates the water beneath, fish and other aquatic organisms survive in lakes during very cold weather.\n\nExample 2: Write equations to show how temporary hardness is caused and how it is removed by boiling.\nSolution: Rainwater dissolves carbon(IV) oxide to form weak trioxocarbonic(IV) acid, which reacts with limestone: CaCO3 plus H2O plus CO2 gives Ca(HCO3)2, which is soluble and causes hardness. On boiling, the reverse occurs: Ca(HCO3)2 gives CaCO3 plus H2O plus CO2. The insoluble calcium trioxocarbonate(IV) settles out as scale or fur, and the water is softened.\n\nExample 3: A sample of water lathers readily with soap after boiling but not before. Identify the type of hardness and justify your answer.\nSolution: The hardness must be temporary hardness. Boiling decomposes the calcium and magnesium hydrogentrioxocarbonates(IV) that cause temporary hardness, removing the offending ions as insoluble trioxocarbonates(IV). Permanent hardness caused by tetraoxosulphates(VI) and chlorides is unaffected by boiling, so if any remained the water would still fail to lather. Since lather forms freely after boiling, the sample contained only temporary hardness.\n\nExample 4: Describe how the permutit process softens hard water and how the permutit is regenerated.\nSolution: Permutit is a sodium aluminium silicate that can exchange its sodium ions for other cations. Hard water is passed slowly through a tank packed with permutit, and the calcium and magnesium ions in the water are exchanged for sodium ions from the permutit. Since sodium salts do not cause hardness, the water leaving the tank is soft. When the permutit is exhausted it is regenerated by flushing it with concentrated brine, which reverses the exchange and restores the sodium ions.\n\nExample 5: State two advantages and two disadvantages of hard water in a laundry business run by Zetra.\nSolution: The advantages are that hard water supplies calcium ions that are good for health if the water is also drunk, and that it tastes better than soft water because of its dissolved mineral salts. The disadvantages are far more serious in a laundry: hard water wastes a great deal of soap because the soap must first react with the calcium and magnesium ions before lather can form, and the insoluble scum produced sticks to fabrics and discolours them. In addition, scale deposits in boilers and heating equipment waste fuel and may cause damage.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Water is a covalent compound with a bent molecular shape.\n- The H-O-H bond angle in water is about 104.5 degrees.\n- Water is polar because oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen.\n- Hydrogen bonding gives water an abnormally high boiling point.\n- Water has a high specific heat capacity and high latent heat.\n- Ice is less dense than water because of its open hydrogen-bonded structure.\n- Water has its maximum density at 4 degrees Celsius.\n- Pure water is neutral with a pH of 7.\n- Water reacts with sodium and potassium to give hydrogen and an alkali.\n- Steam reacts with hot iron to give iron oxide and hydrogen.\n- Water reacts with basic oxides to form alkalis.\n- Water reacts with acidic oxides to form acids.\n- Anhydrous copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) turns blue with water.\n- Anhydrous cobalt(II) chloride turns from blue to pink with water.\n- Purity of water is confirmed by boiling point 100 and freezing point 0 degrees Celsius.\n- Town water treatment includes sedimentation, filtration and chlorination.\n- Alum is used as a coagulant in water treatment.\n- Hardness prevents water from lathering readily with soap.\n- Hardness is caused by dissolved calcium and magnesium salts.\n- Temporary hardness is due to hydrogentrioxocarbonates(IV).\n- Permanent hardness is due to tetraoxosulphates(VI) and chlorides.\n- Temporary hardness is removed by boiling.\n- Clark\'s method uses calculated amounts of calcium hydroxide.\n- Permanent hardness is not removed by boiling.\n- Washing soda removes both types of hardness by precipitation.\n- The permutit process exchanges calcium and magnesium for sodium ions.\n- Exhausted permutit is regenerated with concentrated brine.\n- Ion-exchange resins can remove all ions to give deionised water.\n- Distillation removes all hardness but is expensive.\n- Hardness wastes soap and forms scum.\n- Scale or fur in kettles and boilers wastes fuel.\n- Boiler scale can cause overheating and explosion.\n- Hard water supplies calcium for bones and teeth.\n- Hard water is valued in brewing and tanning.\n- Hard water coats lead pipes and reduces lead poisoning.\n- Soap solution is used to test for and compare degrees of hardness.',
