    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Air and Combustion',
    'body': 'Air is a homogeneous mixture of gases surrounding the earth. Its approximate composition by volume is nitrogen 78 per cent, oxygen 21 per cent, noble gases about 0.9 per cent, carbon(IV) oxide about 0.03 per cent, together with variable amounts of water vapour, dust and pollutants. The evidence that air is a mixture rather than a compound is strong: its components are present in variable proportions, they can be separated by physical means such as fractional distillation, no energy change occurs when the gases are mixed, and the properties of air are the sum of the properties of its components.\n\nThe percentage of oxygen in air can be determined by passing air repeatedly over heated copper in a closed system, since copper combines with oxygen to form black copper(II) oxide and the volume of the gas decreases by about one fifth. Alternatively, moist iron filings or an alkaline solution of pyrogallol may be used, or a candle burning in a closed jar over water. Nitrogen is obtained industrially by the fractional distillation of liquid air: air is filtered, carbon(IV) oxide and water vapour are removed, the air is compressed and cooled until it liquefies, and the liquid is then allowed to warm so that nitrogen, boiling at minus 196 degrees Celsius, distils off before oxygen at minus 183 degrees Celsius.\n\nOxygen is the most important reactive component. It is prepared in the laboratory by the catalytic decomposition of hydrogen peroxide using manganese(IV) oxide, or by heating potassium trioxochlorate(V) with manganese(IV) oxide. It is a colourless, odourless gas, slightly soluble in water, and neutral to litmus. Oxygen relights a glowing splint, which is its standard test. It supports combustion but does not itself burn; metals burn in it to give basic oxides while non-metals give acidic oxides. Its uses include respiration, welding with ethyne in the oxy-acetylene flame, steel making, rocket propulsion and medical oxygen therapy.\n\nCombustion is the rapid reaction of a substance with oxygen accompanied by the evolution of heat and usually light. Complete combustion of a hydrocarbon in excess oxygen produces carbon(IV) oxide and water only; incomplete combustion in a limited supply produces carbon(II) oxide and soot, wastes fuel and is extremely dangerous because carbon(II) oxide is a colourless, odourless, poisonous gas that combines irreversibly with haemoglobin to form carboxyhaemoglobin. Kindling temperature or ignition temperature is the minimum temperature to which a substance must be heated before it will burn, and a fire requires three things, namely fuel, oxygen and heat, forming the fire triangle; removing any one extinguishes the fire. Water cools and excludes air, sand smothers small fires, carbon(IV) oxide extinguishers are used for electrical and oil fires, and foam extinguishers are used for petroleum fires. Water must never be used on electrical or oil fires.\n\nRusting is slow oxidation of iron in the presence of both oxygen and water, forming hydrated iron(III) oxide. Respiration is the slow, controlled oxidation of glucose in living cells to give carbon(IV) oxide, water and energy, effectively the reverse of photosynthesis.\n\nAir pollution results from carbon(II) oxide from vehicles, sulphur(IV) oxide from burning fossil fuels containing sulphur, oxides of nitrogen from high-temperature combustion, unburnt hydrocarbons, lead compounds and particulates. Sulphur and nitrogen oxides dissolve in rain to give acid rain, which corrodes buildings, acidifies lakes and damages crops. Excess carbon(IV) oxide contributes to the greenhouse effect and global warming, while chlorofluorocarbons deplete the ozone layer that shields the earth from ultraviolet radiation. Control measures include catalytic converters, use of low-sulphur fuels, scrubbing of industrial gases, afforestation and the substitution of cleaner energy sources.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: Give three reasons why air is regarded as a mixture and not a compound.\nSolution: First, the composition of air is not fixed; the proportions of water vapour, carbon(IV) oxide and dust vary from place to place. Second, the components of air can be separated by purely physical means, notably fractional distillation of liquid air. Third, no appreciable heat change occurs when the gases of air are mixed together, whereas compound formation involves a definite energy change. In addition, air shows the combined properties of its individual components.\n\nExample 2: Describe an experiment to determine the percentage of oxygen in air using copper.\nSolution: A known volume of air is enclosed between two gas syringes joined by a silica tube containing copper turnings. The copper is heated strongly and the air is passed backwards and forwards over it repeatedly so that all the oxygen reacts to form black copper(II) oxide. The apparatus is cooled to room temperature and the final volume of gas is measured. The decrease in volume, expressed as a percentage of the original volume, gives about 21 per cent, which is the percentage of oxygen in air.\n\nExample 3: Explain why carbon(II) oxide is described as a dangerous pollutant.\nSolution: Carbon(II) oxide is produced by incomplete combustion of carbon-containing fuels in a limited supply of air, for example in generators and charcoal stoves used indoors. It is colourless and odourless, so its presence cannot be detected by the senses and people are overcome without warning. It combines with haemoglobin far more strongly than oxygen does, forming stable carboxyhaemoglobin and preventing the blood from transporting oxygen. The result is oxygen starvation of the tissues, unconsciousness and death.\n\nExample 4: Write balanced equations for the complete and incomplete combustion of methane and state one visible difference.\nSolution: Complete combustion is CH4 plus 2O2 gives CO2 plus 2H2O, and it occurs in excess air with a clean blue, non-luminous flame. Incomplete combustion may be written 2CH4 plus 3O2 gives 2CO plus 4H2O, and it occurs in a limited supply of air. The incomplete reaction gives a yellow, luminous, sooty flame because unburnt carbon particles glow. It also releases less heat per mole of fuel and produces poisonous carbon(II) oxide.\n\nExample 5: Explain how acid rain is formed and state two of its effects.\nSolution: Sulphur(IV) oxide from burning sulphur-containing fossil fuels and oxides of nitrogen from high-temperature engine combustion escape into the atmosphere. There they are oxidised and dissolve in rainwater to form tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid and trioxonitrate(V) acid, giving rain with a pH well below 5.6. This acid rain corrodes limestone and marble buildings and metal structures, and it lowers the pH of lakes and rivers so that fish and other aquatic organisms die. It also leaches nutrients from soils and damages crops and forests.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Air is a homogeneous mixture of gases.\n- Air is about 78 per cent nitrogen and 21 per cent oxygen by volume.\n- Carbon(IV) oxide makes up about 0.03 per cent of air.\n- Noble gases make up about 0.9 per cent of air, mostly argon.\n- Water vapour and dust are variable components of air.\n- Air is a mixture because composition varies and no energy change occurs on mixing.\n- Components of air are separated by fractional distillation of liquid air.\n- Nitrogen boils at minus 196 degrees Celsius and distils off before oxygen.\n- Oxygen boils at minus 183 degrees Celsius.\n- Oxygen percentage can be measured by passing air over heated copper.\n- Alkaline pyrogallol absorbs oxygen from air.\n- Oxygen relights a glowing splint.\n- Oxygen supports combustion but does not burn.\n- Metals burn in oxygen to form basic oxides.\n- Non-metals burn in oxygen to form acidic oxides.\n- Hydrogen peroxide with manganese(IV) oxide gives oxygen in the laboratory.\n- Nitrogen is largely unreactive and is used to provide inert atmospheres.\n- Nitrogen is used industrially to manufacture ammonia.\n- Combustion is rapid oxidation with the release of heat and light.\n- Complete combustion of hydrocarbons gives carbon(IV) oxide and water.\n- Incomplete combustion gives carbon(II) oxide and soot.\n- Carbon(II) oxide is colourless, odourless and highly poisonous.\n- Carbon(II) oxide forms carboxyhaemoglobin with blood.\n- Kindling temperature is the minimum temperature for burning to begin.\n- The fire triangle consists of fuel, oxygen and heat.\n- Removing any side of the fire triangle extinguishes a fire.\n- Carbon(IV) oxide extinguishers suit electrical and oil fires.\n- Water must not be used on electrical or oil fires.\n- Rusting is slow oxidation requiring both air and water.\n- Respiration is slow oxidation of glucose releasing energy.\n- Photosynthesis removes carbon(IV) oxide and releases oxygen.\n- Sulphur(IV) oxide and nitrogen oxides cause acid rain.\n- Acid rain corrodes buildings and acidifies lakes.\n- Excess carbon(IV) oxide contributes to global warming.\n- Chlorofluorocarbons deplete the ozone layer.\n- Catalytic converters and clean fuels reduce air pollution.',
