    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Extraction of Metals and Metallurgy',
    'body': 'Metallurgy is the science of extracting metals from their ores and refining them for use. A mineral is a naturally occurring compound of a metal, while an ore is a mineral from which the metal can be extracted profitably. Important ores include haematite, iron(III) oxide, and magnetite for iron; bauxite, hydrated aluminium oxide, for aluminium; copper pyrites and malachite for copper; zinc blende and calamine for zinc; galena, lead(II) sulphide, for lead; and rock salt for sodium.\n\nThe method of extraction depends on the position of the metal in the activity or electrochemical series, because this determines the stability of its compounds. Very reactive metals such as potassium, sodium, calcium, magnesium and aluminium form very stable compounds and are extracted by electrolysis of their molten salts or oxides. Moderately reactive metals such as zinc, iron, tin and lead are extracted by reduction of their oxides with carbon or carbon(II) oxide. Metals low in the series, such as copper, silver and mercury, occur partly native and their oxides decompose on gentle heating, so they are obtained by roasting or simple heating.\n\nExtraction generally follows four stages. Concentration or dressing removes gangue, the unwanted rocky material, by physical methods such as froth flotation for sulphide ores, magnetic separation, washing or chemical treatment as in the Bayer process for bauxite. Roasting or calcination converts sulphide or carbonate ores into oxides, since oxides are more easily reduced. Reduction converts the oxide into the metal, using carbon, carbon(II) oxide, hydrogen, a more reactive metal, or electrolysis. Finally, refining purifies the crude metal by electrolysis, distillation, liquation or zone refining.\n\nIron is extracted in the blast furnace from a charge of haematite, coke and limestone, with hot air blasted in at the base. The coke burns to carbon(IV) oxide, which is reduced by more coke to carbon(II) oxide, and this gas is the principal reducing agent: Fe2O3 plus 3CO gives 2Fe plus 3CO2. Limestone decomposes to calcium oxide, which combines with silicon(IV) oxide impurities to form calcium trioxosilicate(IV), the slag, which floats on the molten iron and is tapped off separately. The product, pig iron or cast iron, contains about 4 per cent carbon and is hard but brittle; it is converted to steel by blowing oxygen through it to burn off excess carbon and adding measured amounts of carbon and other elements.\n\nAluminium is extracted by the Hall-Heroult process. Purified alumina is dissolved in molten cryolite to lower the working temperature to about 950 degrees Celsius, and electrolysed in a steel tank lined with graphite that acts as the cathode. Aluminium collects at the bottom while oxygen is liberated at the carbon anodes, which burn away and are replaced regularly. The process consumes enormous quantities of electricity, so smelters are sited near cheap power.\n\nAlloys are homogeneous mixtures of a metal with one or more other elements, made to improve hardness, strength, corrosion resistance or appearance. Steel is iron with carbon; stainless steel adds chromium and nickel; brass is copper and zinc; bronze is copper and tin; duralumin is aluminium with copper, magnesium and manganese; and solder is tin and lead. Corrosion is prevented by painting, oiling, galvanising, electroplating, alloying, and cathodic protection. Nigeria has significant deposits of iron ore at Itakpe, tin at Jos, lead and zinc in the south-east, and columbite and coal, and their exploitation supports local industry.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: Explain why sodium cannot be extracted by reduction of its oxide with carbon.\nSolution: Sodium is very high in the activity series and has a strong affinity for oxygen, so sodium oxide is extremely stable. Carbon is a weaker reducing agent than sodium itself, so it cannot remove oxygen from sodium oxide at attainable temperatures. In fact sodium would reduce carbon compounds rather than the reverse. Sodium is therefore extracted by electrolysis of its fused chloride in the Downs cell, where electrical energy supplies the necessary reducing power.\n\nExample 2: Write the three main reactions occurring in the blast furnace and state the role of limestone.\nSolution: First, coke burns in the hot air blast: C plus O2 gives CO2, providing heat. Second, the carbon(IV) oxide is reduced by more coke: CO2 plus C gives 2CO. Third, carbon(II) oxide reduces the ore: Fe2O3 plus 3CO gives 2Fe plus 3CO2. Limestone decomposes to CaO, which reacts with sandy impurities, CaO plus SiO2 giving CaSiO3, the molten slag that removes the gangue and floats on the iron.\n\nExample 3: Why is cryolite added during the extraction of aluminium?\nSolution: Pure aluminium oxide melts at over 2000 degrees Celsius, which would make electrolysis prohibitively expensive and technically difficult. Dissolving the alumina in molten cryolite, sodium hexafluoroaluminate, lowers the operating temperature to about 950 degrees Celsius. This saves a great deal of energy and reduces wear on the cell lining. The cryolite also increases the electrical conductivity of the melt.\n\nExample 4: Distinguish between cast iron, wrought iron and steel in terms of carbon content and properties.\nSolution: Cast iron or pig iron contains about 3 to 4.5 per cent carbon and is hard, brittle and cannot be forged, so it is used for engine blocks, manhole covers and railings. Wrought iron contains less than 0.2 per cent carbon and is soft, malleable and tough, so it is used for gates, chains and ornamental work. Steel contains between about 0.2 and 1.5 per cent carbon and combines strength with the ability to be forged and tempered, so it is used for tools, rails and structures. Increasing carbon content increases hardness but decreases malleability.\n\nExample 5: State three reasons why alloys are preferred to pure metals, giving an example in each case.\nSolution: Alloys are usually harder and stronger than the pure metals, so steel is used in construction while pure iron would be too soft. Alloys often resist corrosion better, so stainless steel containing chromium and nickel is used for cutlery and surgical instruments. Alloys can also have a lower melting point or better appearance, so solder, an alloy of tin and lead, melts at a low temperature for joining electrical components, and brass has an attractive gold-like lustre for fittings.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- A mineral is a naturally occurring compound of a metal.\n- An ore is a mineral from which a metal can be profitably extracted.\n- Haematite and magnetite are ores of iron.\n- Bauxite is the chief ore of aluminium.\n- Zinc blende is zinc sulphide and galena is lead(II) sulphide.\n- The extraction method depends on the metal\'s position in the activity series.\n- Very reactive metals are extracted by electrolysis of molten compounds.\n- Moderately reactive metals are extracted by reduction with carbon.\n- Unreactive metals occur native or are obtained by simple roasting.\n- Concentration removes gangue from the ore.\n- Froth flotation is used to concentrate sulphide ores.\n- Roasting converts sulphide ores to oxides.\n- Calcination converts carbonate ores to oxides.\n- Oxides are more easily reduced than sulphides or carbonates.\n- The blast furnace uses ore, coke, limestone and hot air.\n- Carbon(II) oxide is the main reducing agent in the blast furnace.\n- Limestone removes silicon(IV) oxide impurities as slag.\n- Slag is calcium trioxosilicate(IV) and floats on molten iron.\n- Pig iron contains about 4 per cent carbon and is brittle.\n- Steel is made by burning off excess carbon with oxygen.\n- Wrought iron has the lowest carbon content and is malleable.\n- Aluminium is extracted by the Hall-Heroult electrolytic process.\n- Cryolite lowers the melting point of alumina and saves energy.\n- Carbon anodes burn away during aluminium extraction and are replaced.\n- Aluminium extraction requires very large amounts of electricity.\n- Copper is refined by electrolysis using impure copper as the anode.\n- Anode mud from copper refining yields silver and gold.\n- Zone refining produces ultra-pure metals for electronics.\n- Alloys are homogeneous mixtures of metals with other elements.\n- Brass is an alloy of copper and zinc.\n- Bronze is an alloy of copper and tin.\n- Duralumin is a light strong alloy of aluminium used in aircraft.\n- Stainless steel contains chromium and nickel and resists corrosion.\n- Alloys are harder, stronger and often more corrosion resistant than pure metals.\n- Galvanising and cathodic protection prevent rusting of iron.\n- Nigeria has ores including iron at Itakpe, tin at Jos and coal at Enugu.',
