    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Environmental and Industrial Chemistry',
    'body': 'Industrial chemistry converts raw materials into useful products on a large scale, while environmental chemistry studies the effect of these and other human activities on air, water and soil. A chemical industry must consider availability of raw materials, energy supply, transport, water, labour, market and, increasingly, waste disposal and pollution control.\n\nPollution is the introduction into the environment of substances that are harmful to living things. Air pollutants include carbon(II) oxide from incomplete combustion, sulphur(IV) oxide from burning sulphur-containing fuels, oxides of nitrogen from vehicle engines, unburnt hydrocarbons, lead compounds from leaded petrol, chlorofluorocarbons from aerosols and refrigerators, and particulates such as soot and cement dust. Their effects include respiratory disease, acid rain, the greenhouse effect and global warming, depletion of the ozone layer, photochemical smog and reduced visibility. Water pollutants include untreated sewage, industrial effluents containing heavy metals such as mercury, lead and cadmium, oil spillage from petroleum operations, detergents and agricultural run-off carrying fertilisers and pesticides. Excess fertiliser causes eutrophication, in which algae grow rapidly, die and are decomposed by bacteria that use up the dissolved oxygen, killing fish. Soil pollution arises from indiscriminate dumping of refuse, non-biodegradable plastics, excessive use of agrochemicals and mining waste.\n\nControl measures include treating industrial effluents before discharge, scrubbing waste gases with alkaline solutions, fitting catalytic converters and electrostatic precipitators, using low-sulphur and unleaded fuels, recycling of metals, glass, paper and plastics, proper waste segregation and landfill management, afforestation, and enforcement of environmental laws by agencies such as NESREA in Nigeria.\n\nFertilisers replace nutrients removed from the soil by crops. The three major nutrients are nitrogen, which promotes leaf and stem growth, phosphorus, which promotes root development and flowering, and potassium, which improves disease resistance and fruit quality. Nitrogenous fertilisers include ammonium tetraoxosulphate(VI), ammonium trioxonitrate(V) and urea; phosphatic fertilisers include superphosphate; potassic fertilisers include potassium chloride; and NPK is a compound fertiliser containing all three. Fertilisers increase yield, but overuse acidifies the soil, leaches into rivers and causes eutrophication.\n\nOther important industrial products include tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid by the contact process, ammonia by the Haber process, trioxonitrate(V) acid by the Ostwald process, sodium hydroxide and chlorine from the electrolysis of brine, sodium trioxocarbonate(IV) by the Solvay process, cement from limestone and clay, glass from sand, limestone and sodium trioxocarbonate(IV), soap by saponification of oils with alkali, detergents from petroleum products, plastics and synthetic fibres by polymerisation, and paints, ceramics and paper. Petroleum refining separates crude oil by fractional distillation into gases, petrol, kerosene, diesel, lubricating oil and bitumen, and cracking converts heavy fractions into more valuable lighter ones and into alkenes for the plastics industry.\n\nAlloys, discussed earlier, are also industrial products. A company such as Zetra planning a fertiliser plant must weigh proximity to natural gas as a hydrogen source, to water and to farming markets, and must plan for effluent treatment. Green chemistry principles encourage atom-efficient reactions, catalytic rather than stoichiometric reagents, safer solvents, renewable feedstocks and biodegradable products.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: Explain the process of eutrophication and its consequences.\nSolution: Fertiliser washed from farmland into a river or lake enriches the water with trioxonitrate(V) and phosphate nutrients. Algae and other water plants multiply rapidly, forming a thick bloom that blocks light from plants below. When the algae die, aerobic bacteria decompose them and consume the dissolved oxygen in the water. Oxygen levels fall so low that fish and other aquatic animals suffocate and die, and the water becomes foul-smelling and unusable.\n\nExample 2: Describe the manufacture of tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid by the contact process, giving the equations.\nSolution: Sulphur or sulphide ore is burnt in air to give sulphur(IV) oxide: S plus O2 gives SO2. The purified, dried gas is mixed with excess air and passed over vanadium(V) oxide catalyst at about 450 degrees Celsius and 2 atmospheres: 2SO2 plus O2 gives 2SO3, a reversible exothermic reaction. The sulphur(VI) oxide is absorbed in concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid to give oleum, H2S2O7, because direct absorption in water produces an uncontrollable mist. The oleum is then diluted carefully with water to give concentrated acid.\n\nExample 3: State three functions of NPK fertiliser components and one disadvantage of overuse.\nSolution: Nitrogen promotes vegetative growth by forming proteins and chlorophyll, giving healthy green leaves. Phosphorus encourages strong root development, early ripening and flowering. Potassium strengthens plant tissue, improves resistance to disease and improves the quality of fruits and tubers. Overuse, however, leaches into water bodies and causes eutrophication, and it can also make the soil acidic and destroy soil organisms.\n\nExample 4: Explain how catalytic converters reduce pollution from motor vehicles.\nSolution: A catalytic converter is fitted in the exhaust system and contains platinum, palladium and rhodium spread over a honeycomb support to give a large surface area. It oxidises carbon(II) oxide to carbon(IV) oxide and unburnt hydrocarbons to carbon(IV) oxide and water. It also reduces oxides of nitrogen to harmless nitrogen gas. The vehicle must use unleaded petrol because lead poisons the catalyst and destroys its activity permanently.\n\nExample 5: Distinguish between soap and detergent and state one advantage of detergents.\nSolution: Soap is the sodium or potassium salt of a long-chain fatty acid, made by boiling fats or oils with sodium hydroxide in a process called saponification. Detergents are sodium salts of sulphonic acids made from petroleum products. Both clean because they have a long non-polar hydrocarbon tail that dissolves grease and an ionic head that dissolves in water. The chief advantage of detergents is that their calcium and magnesium salts are soluble, so they lather readily in hard water and do not form scum, although non-biodegradable detergents can pollute rivers.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Pollution is the release of harmful substances into the environment.\n- Carbon(II) oxide arises from incomplete combustion and is poisonous.\n- Sulphur(IV) oxide from fossil fuels causes acid rain.\n- Oxides of nitrogen are formed in high-temperature engine combustion.\n- Chlorofluorocarbons destroy stratospheric ozone.\n- The ozone layer shields the earth from ultraviolet radiation.\n- Excess carbon(IV) oxide causes the greenhouse effect.\n- Global warming raises sea levels and disturbs rainfall patterns.\n- Particulates such as soot and cement dust harm the lungs.\n- Water pollution sources include sewage, effluents and oil spills.\n- Heavy metals such as mercury and lead accumulate in the food chain.\n- Eutrophication is caused by nutrient enrichment of water bodies.\n- Eutrophication depletes dissolved oxygen and kills fish.\n- Oil spillage destroys aquatic life and farmland.\n- Soil pollution results from refuse, plastics and agrochemicals.\n- Non-biodegradable plastics persist for many years.\n- Recycling conserves resources and reduces waste.\n- Effluent treatment must precede discharge into water bodies.\n- Scrubbers remove acidic gases from industrial emissions.\n- Electrostatic precipitators remove dust from flue gases.\n- Catalytic converters reduce carbon(II) oxide, hydrocarbons and nitrogen oxides.\n- Leaded petrol poisons catalytic converters and harms children.\n- Fertilisers replace nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium in soil.\n- Nitrogen promotes leaf and stem growth.\n- Phosphorus promotes root growth and flowering.\n- Potassium improves disease resistance and fruit quality.\n- Urea and ammonium salts are nitrogenous fertilisers.\n- NPK fertiliser supplies all three major nutrients.\n- Ammonia is manufactured by the Haber process.\n- Tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid is manufactured by the contact process.\n- Trioxonitrate(V) acid is manufactured by the Ostwald process.\n- Sodium hydroxide and chlorine come from electrolysis of brine.\n- Sodium trioxocarbonate(IV) is made by the Solvay process.\n- Cement is made from limestone and clay.\n- Soap is made by saponification of oils with alkali.\n- Detergents lather in hard water because their calcium salts are soluble.\n- Petroleum is refined by fractional distillation and cracking.\n- Green chemistry promotes safer, cleaner and more efficient processes.',
