    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Introduction to Organic Chemistry and Hydrocarbons',
    'body': 'Organic chemistry is the chemistry of carbon compounds, excluding the oxides of carbon, trioxocarbonates(IV), hydrogentrioxocarbonates(IV) and a few others which are treated as inorganic. Carbon forms an enormous number of compounds because of two special properties: catenation, the ability of carbon atoms to link with one another in chains and rings of unlimited length, and tetravalency, the ability to form four strong covalent bonds. Carbon also forms multiple bonds with itself and with oxygen and nitrogen.\n\nA homologous series is a family of organic compounds with the same general formula, in which successive members differ by a CH2 unit, which have similar chemical properties and show a gradual change in physical properties, and which can be prepared by similar general methods. A functional group is the atom or group of atoms responsible for the characteristic chemical properties of a series. Isomerism is the existence of two or more compounds with the same molecular formula but different structural formulae; chain, positional and functional isomerism are the common types, so butane and 2-methylpropane are chain isomers, while but-1-ene and but-2-ene are positional isomers.\n\nHydrocarbons contain hydrogen and carbon only. Alkanes have the general formula CnH2n+2 and contain only single bonds, so they are described as saturated. The first members are methane, ethane, propane and butane. They are obtained from natural gas and petroleum, and their physical properties change gradually: the first four are gases, the next are liquids and the higher members are waxy solids, while boiling point increases with molecular size because van der Waals forces increase. Alkanes are relatively unreactive, but they burn in excess air to give carbon(IV) oxide and water and undergo substitution with chlorine in the presence of sunlight, methane giving chloromethane and eventually tetrachloromethane. Cracking of long-chain alkanes gives shorter alkanes and alkenes.\n\nAlkenes have the general formula CnH2n and contain one carbon-to-carbon double bond, making them unsaturated. Ethene is the simplest and is prepared by cracking, by dehydration of ethanol with concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, or by elimination of hydrogen halide from a halogenoalkane using alcoholic potassium hydroxide. Alkenes are far more reactive than alkanes and undergo addition reactions: with hydrogen and a nickel catalyst they form alkanes, a reaction used to harden vegetable oils into margarine; with bromine water the reddish-brown colour is decolourised, which is the standard test for unsaturation; with hydrogen halides they form halogenoalkanes; with steam and an acid catalyst they form alcohols; and with acidified potassium tetraoxomanganate(VII) they are oxidised to diols, decolourising the purple solution. Ethene also polymerises to poly(ethene), an addition polymer.\n\nAlkynes have the general formula CnH2n-2 and contain a triple bond. Ethyne, the simplest, is prepared by the action of water on calcium dicarbide, giving ethyne and calcium hydroxide. It burns in oxygen with a very hot flame used in the oxy-acetylene torch for welding and cutting metals, and it undergoes addition reactions similar to those of alkenes but adding two molecules of reagent.\n\nBenzene, C6H6, is the parent aromatic hydrocarbon, with six carbon atoms in a planar ring and delocalised electrons above and below the plane. Because of this stability, benzene undergoes substitution rather than addition, giving nitrobenzene with a mixture of concentrated trioxonitrate(V) and tetraoxosulphate(VI) acids, and chlorobenzene with chlorine and a halogen carrier.\n\nNomenclature follows IUPAC rules: identify the longest carbon chain, number it so that the functional group or substituent gets the lowest possible number, name the substituents alphabetically with their position numbers, and use the suffix -ane, -ene or -yne appropriately.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: Write the equation for the complete combustion of propane and calculate the volume of oxygen required to burn 10 cm3 of propane.\nSolution: The balanced equation is C3H8 plus 5O2 gives 3CO2 plus 4H2O. By Avogadro\'s law the volume ratio equals the mole ratio, so one volume of propane requires five volumes of oxygen. Therefore 10 cm3 of propane requires 10 times 5, which is 50 cm3 of oxygen. The reaction would also produce 30 cm3 of carbon(IV) oxide measured at the same conditions.\n\nExample 2: Describe a simple chemical test to distinguish between ethane and ethene.\nSolution: Bubble each gas separately through bromine water in a test tube. Ethene, being unsaturated, adds bromine across its double bond to form 1,2-dibromoethane, and the reddish-brown colour of the bromine water is rapidly decolourised. Ethane is saturated and has no double bond, so the bromine water retains its colour in the dark. Alternatively, ethene decolourises acidified potassium tetraoxomanganate(VII) while ethane does not.\n\nExample 3: Draw and name the structural isomers of C4H10 and state one difference in their properties.\nSolution: There are two isomers. The first is butane, an unbranched chain of four carbon atoms, CH3CH2CH2CH3. The second is 2-methylpropane, a three-carbon chain with a methyl group on the middle carbon. They have the same molecular formula but different structures, so they are chain isomers. Butane has the higher boiling point because its unbranched molecules pack together more closely, giving stronger van der Waals forces.\n\nExample 4: Explain how ethanol can be converted to ethene and how ethene can be converted back to ethanol.\nSolution: Ethanol is dehydrated by heating with excess concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid at about 170 degrees Celsius, or by passing its vapour over heated aluminium oxide; a molecule of water is eliminated and ethene is formed. The reverse conversion is the industrial hydration of ethene: ethene and steam are passed over phosphoric(V) acid catalyst at about 300 degrees Celsius and 60 atmospheres, and water adds across the double bond to give ethanol. The two processes illustrate elimination and addition respectively.\n\nExample 5: State what is meant by polymerisation and describe the formation of poly(ethene).\nSolution: Polymerisation is the joining together of many small molecules called monomers to form a very large molecule called a polymer. In addition polymerisation the monomers add together without the loss of any small molecule. Ethene molecules, under high pressure and temperature with a suitable catalyst, open their double bonds and link end to end to give a long saturated chain, poly(ethene). The polymer is flexible, chemically inert and used for bags, bottles and insulation, but it is non-biodegradable and contributes to environmental pollution.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Organic chemistry is the study of carbon compounds.\n- Catenation is the ability of carbon to bond to itself in chains and rings.\n- Carbon is tetravalent and forms four covalent bonds.\n- A homologous series has a general formula and a common functional group.\n- Successive members of a homologous series differ by CH2.\n- Members of a homologous series show gradual change in physical properties.\n- Isomers have the same molecular formula but different structures.\n- Chain, positional and functional isomerism are common types.\n- Alkanes have the general formula CnH2n+2 and are saturated.\n- The first four alkanes are methane, ethane, propane and butane.\n- Alkanes undergo substitution reactions with halogens in sunlight.\n- Alkanes burn in excess air to give carbon(IV) oxide and water.\n- Alkanes are relatively unreactive because of strong single bonds.\n- Boiling points of alkanes increase with chain length.\n- Branched isomers have lower boiling points than straight-chain isomers.\n- Cracking breaks large alkanes into smaller alkanes and alkenes.\n- Alkenes have the general formula CnH2n and contain a double bond.\n- Alkenes are unsaturated and undergo addition reactions.\n- Ethene decolourises bromine water, the test for unsaturation.\n- Ethene with hydrogen and nickel gives ethane.\n- Hydrogenation of vegetable oil produces margarine.\n- Hydration of ethene with steam and acid catalyst gives ethanol.\n- Dehydration of ethanol with concentrated acid gives ethene.\n- Ethene polymerises to poly(ethene).\n- Addition polymers form without loss of small molecules.\n- Alkynes have the general formula CnH2n-2 and contain a triple bond.\n- Ethyne is prepared from calcium dicarbide and water.\n- The oxy-acetylene flame is used for welding and cutting metals.\n- Benzene is the simplest aromatic hydrocarbon with formula C6H6.\n- Benzene has delocalised electrons and undergoes substitution.\n- Nitration of benzene gives nitrobenzene.\n- IUPAC names use the longest chain and lowest locant numbers.\n- Suffixes -ane, -ene and -yne indicate single, double and triple bonds.\n- Petroleum is a mixture of hydrocarbons separated by fractional distillation.\n- Petrol, kerosene and diesel are petroleum fractions.\n- Non-biodegradable plastics from alkenes cause environmental problems.',
