    'subject': 'Chemistry',
    'chapterTitle': 'Alcohols and Carboxylic Acids',
    'body': 'Alkanols, commonly called alcohols, are organic compounds containing the hydroxyl functional group, -OH, attached to a saturated carbon atom. Their general formula is CnH2n+1OH, and the members include methanol, ethanol, propanol and butanol. Alcohols are classified as primary, secondary or tertiary according to whether the carbon bearing the hydroxyl group is attached to one, two or three other carbon atoms. They may also be monohydric, dihydric or trihydric according to the number of hydroxyl groups; ethane-1,2-diol used as antifreeze is dihydric, and propane-1,2,3-triol, or glycerol, is trihydric.\n\nBecause of the polar O-H bond, alcohols form hydrogen bonds with one another and with water. This gives them much higher boiling points than alkanes of comparable molar mass and makes the lower members completely miscible with water, although solubility falls as the hydrocarbon chain lengthens. Ethanol is a colourless, volatile liquid with a characteristic smell, boiling at 78 degrees Celsius, and is a good solvent for many organic substances.\n\nEthanol is prepared in the laboratory and industrially by two main methods. Fermentation uses the enzyme complex in yeast to convert sugars into ethanol and carbon(IV) oxide at about 37 degrees Celsius in the absence of air; the process stops at about 14 per cent ethanol because higher concentrations kill the yeast, and the ethanol is concentrated by fractional distillation. Hydration of ethene with steam over phosphoric(V) acid catalyst gives ethanol directly and is used where petroleum is cheap.\n\nThe chemical reactions of alcohols are extensively examined. With sodium metal, ethanol releases hydrogen and forms sodium ethoxide, showing that the hydroxyl hydrogen is slightly acidic, though alcohols are far weaker acids than water and do not affect litmus. Oxidation with acidified potassium heptaoxodichromate(VI) or tetraoxomanganate(VII) converts a primary alcohol first to an aldehyde and then to a carboxylic acid, secondary alcohols to ketones, while tertiary alcohols resist oxidation. Dehydration with concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid at 170 degrees Celsius gives an alkene, or at 140 degrees Celsius gives an ether. Esterification with a carboxylic acid in the presence of a little concentrated acid catalyst gives a sweet-smelling ester and water, a reversible reaction. Ethanol also burns with a clean blue flame, and reacts with hydrogen halides to give halogenoalkanes. The iodoform or triiodomethane test, using iodine and sodium hydroxide, gives a yellow precipitate with ethanol and with secondary alcohols having the CH3CH(OH) grouping.\n\nAlkanoic acids, or carboxylic acids, contain the carboxyl group, -COOH, with general formula CnH2n+1COOH. Methanoic acid occurs in ant stings, ethanoic acid in vinegar, and the higher members such as palmitic and stearic acids occur in fats and oils. They are weak acids, ionising only partially in water, but they show all typical acid reactions: they turn blue litmus red, react with reactive metals to give hydrogen, with bases to give salt and water, and with trioxocarbonates(IV) and hydrogentrioxocarbonates(IV) to give carbon(IV) oxide, which distinguishes them from alcohols and phenols. They form esters with alcohols, and can be reduced to primary alcohols.\n\nEsters are sweet-smelling neutral liquids used as flavourings, perfumes and solvents. Hydrolysis of an ester with acid reverses esterification, while hydrolysis with alkali is irreversible and is called saponification, producing soap and glycerol when the ester is a fat or oil. Fats and oils are esters of glycerol with long-chain fatty acids; fats are mainly saturated and solid, while oils are unsaturated and liquid and can be hardened by hydrogenation.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: Write the equation for the fermentation of glucose and calculate the mass of ethanol obtainable from 90 g of glucose. (C equals 12, H equals 1, O equals 16.)\nSolution: The equation is C6H12O6 gives 2C2H5OH plus 2CO2. The molar mass of glucose is 180 g and that of ethanol is 46 g, and one mole of glucose gives two moles of ethanol. Moles of glucose equal 90 divided by 180, which is 0.5 mol, so moles of ethanol equal 1.0 mol. The mass of ethanol is therefore 1.0 times 46, that is 46 g, assuming complete conversion.\n\nExample 2: Describe a chemical test that distinguishes ethanol from ethanoic acid.\nSolution: Add a small quantity of sodium trioxocarbonate(IV) solution or solid sodium hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV) to each liquid in separate test tubes. Ethanoic acid, being a carboxylic acid, reacts to give brisk effervescence of carbon(IV) oxide, which turns lime water milky. Ethanol gives no reaction at all because the hydroxyl group is not sufficiently acidic. Both, however, would react with sodium metal to give hydrogen, so that test alone is not conclusive.\n\nExample 3: Explain what happens when ethanol is warmed with ethanoic acid in the presence of a few drops of concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid.\nSolution: The two react in an esterification reaction to form ethyl ethanoate and water, with a characteristic sweet, fruity smell. The concentrated acid acts as a catalyst and also as a dehydrating agent, removing water and shifting the equilibrium towards the ester. The reaction is reversible, so the yield is never complete. The equation is CH3COOH plus C2H5OH giving CH3COOC2H5 plus H2O.\n\nExample 4: A primary alcohol is oxidised with acidified potassium heptaoxodichromate(VI). State the observations and the products.\nSolution: The orange colour of the heptaoxodichromate(VI) changes to green as chromium is reduced from the plus 6 to the plus 3 oxidation state. If the product is distilled off as it forms, an aldehyde is obtained, for example ethanol gives ethanal. If the mixture is heated under reflux with excess oxidising agent, oxidation continues and the carboxylic acid, ethanoic acid, is produced. A tertiary alcohol under the same conditions would give no colour change.\n\nExample 5: Explain saponification and how soap cleans greasy cloth.\nSolution: Saponification is the alkaline hydrolysis of a fat or oil, which is an ester of glycerol, by boiling with sodium hydroxide, giving the sodium salts of the fatty acids, that is soap, together with glycerol. A soap molecule has a long hydrocarbon tail that is non-polar and a carboxylate head that is ionic. When the cloth is washed, the tails dissolve in the grease while the heads remain in the water, so the grease is lifted off and broken into tiny droplets. Agitation disperses these droplets as an emulsion that is rinsed away with the water.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Alkanols contain the hydroxyl functional group, -OH.\n- The general formula of alkanols is CnH2n+1OH.\n- Alcohols are classified as primary, secondary or tertiary.\n- Ethane-1,2-diol is dihydric and used as antifreeze.\n- Glycerol is a trihydric alcohol found in fats and oils.\n- Hydrogen bonding gives alcohols high boiling points.\n- Lower alcohols are completely miscible with water.\n- Solubility of alcohols decreases as chain length increases.\n- Ethanol boils at 78 degrees Celsius.\n- Fermentation of sugars by yeast produces ethanol and carbon(IV) oxide.\n- Fermentation stops at about 14 per cent ethanol.\n- Ethanol is concentrated by fractional distillation.\n- Industrial ethanol is also made by hydration of ethene.\n- Alcohols react with sodium to give hydrogen and an alkoxide.\n- Alcohols are neutral to litmus.\n- Primary alcohols oxidise to aldehydes and then to carboxylic acids.\n- Secondary alcohols oxidise to ketones.\n- Tertiary alcohols resist oxidation.\n- Acidified heptaoxodichromate(VI) turns orange to green on oxidation.\n- Dehydration of ethanol at 170 degrees Celsius gives ethene.\n- Alcohols react with acids to form esters.\n- The iodoform test gives a yellow precipitate with ethanol.\n- Methylated spirit is ethanol made unfit to drink.\n- Alkanoic acids contain the carboxyl group, -COOH.\n- Ethanoic acid is the acid present in vinegar.\n- Methanoic acid occurs in ant and bee stings.\n- Carboxylic acids are weak acids and ionise partially.\n- Carboxylic acids liberate carbon(IV) oxide from trioxocarbonates(IV).\n- The trioxocarbonate(IV) test distinguishes acids from alcohols.\n- Carboxylic acids neutralise bases to form salt and water.\n- Esters are sweet-smelling and used in perfumes and flavourings.\n- Esterification is reversible and catalysed by concentrated acid.\n- Acid hydrolysis of an ester gives back acid and alcohol.\n- Alkaline hydrolysis of an ester is called saponification.\n- Saponification of fats gives soap and glycerol.\n- Fats are saturated esters and are solid at room temperature.\n- Oils are unsaturated esters and are liquid at room temperature.\n- Hydrogenation of oils produces margarine.\n- Soap cleans by emulsifying grease with its hydrocarbon tail and ionic head.',
