    'chapterTitle': 'Nitrogen and Its Compounds',
    'body': 'Nitrogen is a Group 5 element with atomic number 7 and electronic configuration 2, 5. It exists as a diatomic gas, N2, in which the two atoms are joined by a very strong triple covalent bond with a bond energy of about 945 kJ per mole. This strong bond explains why nitrogen is so unreactive at ordinary temperatures, and why the fixation of nitrogen requires either high temperature and pressure with a catalyst, as in the Haber process, the energy of lightning, or the specialised enzymes of nitrogen-fixing bacteria in the root nodules of leguminous plants.\n\nNitrogen makes up about 78 per cent of the atmosphere and is obtained industrially by fractional distillation of liquid air. In the laboratory it is prepared by removing oxygen, carbon(IV) oxide and water vapour from air, or more purely by gently heating a solution of ammonium chloride with sodium trioxonitrate(III). It is a colourless, odourless, tasteless gas, slightly less dense than air, sparingly soluble in water and neutral to litmus. It neither burns nor supports combustion, so a lighted splint is extinguished in it. It is used to provide inert atmospheres in food packaging and in the chemical industry, in the manufacture of ammonia, and as liquid nitrogen for refrigeration and preservation of biological specimens.\n\nAmmonia, NH3, is the most important compound of nitrogen. It is manufactured by the Haber process from nitrogen and hydrogen at about 200 atmospheres and 450 to 500 degrees Celsius over an iron catalyst, and is prepared in the laboratory by warming any ammonium salt with a strong alkali such as calcium hydroxide. It is dried with quicklime, not with concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid or calcium chloride, because these react with it, and it is collected by downward displacement of air because it is lighter than air and extremely soluble in water. Ammonia is a colourless gas with a pungent, choking smell; it turns damp red litmus blue, and it gives dense white fumes of ammonium chloride with hydrogen chloride, the standard test. Its extreme solubility is demonstrated by the fountain experiment. Its aqueous solution is a weak base, and it precipitates metal hydroxides from solutions of their salts, redissolving the precipitates of copper, zinc and silver in excess ammonia because soluble complex ions form. Ammonia is oxidised by heated copper(II) oxide to nitrogen, and by oxygen over a platinum catalyst to nitrogen(II) oxide, the first stage of the Ostwald process. Its uses include the manufacture of trioxonitrate(V) acid and fertilisers, as a refrigerant, and in the Solvay process.\n\nTrioxonitrate(V) acid, HNO3, is manufactured by the Ostwald process, in which ammonia is catalytically oxidised to nitrogen(II) oxide, which is further oxidised in air to nitrogen(IV) oxide and absorbed in water in the presence of air to give the acid. It is a strong monobasic acid, but it is also a powerful oxidising agent, so with most metals it does not liberate hydrogen; instead the dilute acid gives nitrogen(II) oxide and the concentrated acid gives brown nitrogen(IV) oxide. Very dilute acid reacts with magnesium to give hydrogen, an exception. Concentrated trioxonitrate(V) acid renders iron and aluminium passive by forming a protective oxide layer. It is used in making fertilisers, explosives such as trinitrotoluene, dyes and in metal etching. Nitrogen(IV) oxide, NO2, is a brown, pungent, poisonous gas that dimerises to N2O4 on cooling and dissolves in water to give a mixture of two acids.\n\nThe nitrogen cycle keeps atmospheric nitrogen and combined nitrogen in balance. Nitrogen is fixed by lightning, by industrial processes and by bacteria; plants absorb trioxonitrate(V) ions and build proteins; animals eat plants; death and excretion return nitrogen compounds to the soil where putrefying bacteria convert them to ammonia, nitrifying bacteria convert ammonia to trioxonitrate(III) and then trioxonitrate(V), and denitrifying bacteria return free nitrogen to the atmosphere.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: Explain why nitrogen gas is chemically unreactive at room temperature.\nSolution: The nitrogen molecule consists of two atoms joined by a triple covalent bond, with three shared pairs of electrons. The bond dissociation energy of about 945 kJ per mole is one of the highest known for any diatomic molecule. A very large amount of energy is therefore required before the molecule can be broken apart to react. At ordinary temperatures this energy is not available, so nitrogen behaves almost as an inert gas.\n\nExample 2: Describe how ammonia is prepared and dried in the laboratory, giving the equation.\nSolution: A mixture of ammonium chloride and slaked lime is heated in a hard glass tube inclined downwards to prevent condensed water running back and cracking it. The equation is 2NH4Cl plus Ca(OH)2 giving CaCl2 plus 2H2O plus 2NH3. The gas is dried by passing it over lumps of quicklime, calcium oxide, because concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid and calcium chloride would both react with the ammonia. It is collected by downward displacement of air since it is less dense than air and far too soluble to be collected over water.\n\nExample 3: Explain what is observed when aqueous ammonia is added gradually until in excess to copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) solution.\nSolution: A pale blue gelatinous precipitate of copper(II) hydroxide forms first, because ammonia solution provides hydroxide ions. On adding more ammonia the precipitate dissolves to give a deep royal blue solution. This is due to the formation of the soluble tetraamminecopper(II) complex ion, in which four ammonia molecules donate lone pairs to the copper ion through dative covalent bonds. The reaction is used as a confirmatory test for copper(II) ions.\n\nExample 4: Why does trioxonitrate(V) acid not normally give hydrogen with metals?\nSolution: Trioxonitrate(V) acid is a strong oxidising agent because nitrogen is in its highest oxidation state of plus 5. Any hydrogen formed when the metal displaces hydrogen ions is immediately oxidised to water. The nitrogen is correspondingly reduced, giving nitrogen(II) oxide with the dilute acid and nitrogen(IV) oxide with the concentrated acid. Only very dilute acid with magnesium or manganese liberates hydrogen, because oxidation is then slow enough to be outrun by the displacement.\n\nExample 5: Outline the nitrogen cycle and state two ways in which human activity affects it.\nSolution: Atmospheric nitrogen is fixed by lightning, by nitrogen-fixing bacteria in legume root nodules and by industrial processes such as the Haber process. Plants absorb trioxonitrate(V) from the soil and convert it into proteins; animals obtain nitrogen by eating plants; and dead organisms and excreta are decomposed by putrefying bacteria to ammonia, which nitrifying bacteria convert into trioxonitrate(V). Denitrifying bacteria return nitrogen gas to the air, completing the cycle. Human activity accelerates the cycle by manufacturing large quantities of fertiliser, which causes eutrophication, and disturbs it by burning fossil fuels, which releases oxides of nitrogen and contributes to acid rain.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Nitrogen has atomic number 7 and configuration 2, 5.\n- Nitrogen exists as diatomic molecules with a triple bond.\n- The strong triple bond makes nitrogen unreactive.\n- Nitrogen forms about 78 per cent of the atmosphere by volume.\n- Nitrogen is obtained industrially by fractional distillation of liquid air.\n- Nitrogen is colourless, odourless and neutral to litmus.\n- Nitrogen neither burns nor supports combustion.\n- Liquid nitrogen is used for refrigeration and specimen preservation.\n- Nitrogen provides an inert atmosphere in food packaging.\n- Ammonia is manufactured by the Haber process.\n- The Haber process uses iron catalyst at about 450 degrees Celsius.\n- Ammonia is prepared in the laboratory from an ammonium salt and an alkali.\n- Ammonia is dried with quicklime, not with acid or calcium chloride.\n- Ammonia is collected by downward displacement of air.\n- Ammonia is extremely soluble in water, shown by the fountain experiment.\n- Ammonia turns damp red litmus paper blue.\n- Ammonia gives dense white fumes with hydrogen chloride.\n- Aqueous ammonia is a weak base.\n- Ammonia precipitates insoluble metal hydroxides from salt solutions.\n- Copper, zinc and silver hydroxides redissolve in excess ammonia.\n- Ammonia is oxidised to nitrogen by heated copper(II) oxide.\n- Catalytic oxidation of ammonia begins the Ostwald process.\n- Trioxonitrate(V) acid is made by the Ostwald process.\n- Trioxonitrate(V) acid is a strong monobasic acid.\n- Trioxonitrate(V) acid is also a strong oxidising agent.\n- Metals with trioxonitrate(V) acid usually give oxides of nitrogen, not hydrogen.\n- Dilute acid gives nitrogen(II) oxide; concentrated acid gives nitrogen(IV) oxide.\n- Concentrated trioxonitrate(V) acid renders iron and aluminium passive.\n- Trioxonitrate(V) acid is used to make fertilisers and explosives.\n- Nitrogen(IV) oxide is a brown, pungent and poisonous gas.\n- All trioxonitrates(V) are soluble in water.\n- Heating trioxonitrates(V) of most metals gives oxide, NO2 and oxygen.\n- Sodium and potassium trioxonitrates(V) give trioxonitrate(III) and oxygen.\n- Ammonium salts sublime or decompose readily on heating.\n- The brown ring test confirms the presence of trioxonitrate(V) ions.\n- The nitrogen cycle recycles nitrogen through air, soil and organisms.\n- Nitrogen fixation occurs by lightning, bacteria and industry.\n- Denitrifying bacteria return free nitrogen to the atmosphere.',
