    'chapterTitle': 'Sulphur and Its Compounds',
    'body': 'Sulphur is a Group 6 element with atomic number 16 and electronic configuration 2, 8, 6. It occurs free in volcanic regions and combined as sulphides such as galena, zinc blende and iron pyrites, and as tetraoxosulphates(VI) such as gypsum and barytes. It is a yellow, brittle, non-metallic solid, insoluble in water but soluble in carbon disulphide, and a poor conductor of heat and electricity.\n\nSulphur shows allotropy, the existence of an element in more than one form in the same physical state. Rhombic sulphur is the stable form below 96 degrees Celsius, has octahedral crystals, a density of about 2.06 g per cm3 and a melting point of 113 degrees Celsius, and is prepared by crystallising a solution of sulphur in carbon disulphide. Monoclinic sulphur is stable between 96 degrees Celsius and its melting point of 119 degrees Celsius, has needle-shaped crystals and a lower density of about 1.98 g per cm3, and is prepared by cooling molten sulphur until a crust forms and pouring off the remaining liquid. The temperature of 96 degrees Celsius is the transition temperature at which the two forms are in equilibrium. Plastic sulphur, an amorphous form, is obtained by pouring boiling sulphur into cold water and slowly reverts to rhombic sulphur.\n\nChemically, sulphur burns in air with a blue flame to give sulphur(IV) oxide. It combines directly with most metals on heating to form sulphides, for example iron gives iron(II) sulphide, and with hydrogen to give hydrogen sulphide. It is oxidised by hot concentrated trioxonitrate(V) acid to tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, and by hot concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid to sulphur(IV) oxide. Sulphur is used in vulcanising rubber, in making tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, matches, gunpowder, fungicides and drugs.\n\nSulphur(IV) oxide, SO2, is prepared by burning sulphur in air, by heating copper with concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, or by adding dilute acid to a trioxosulphate(IV). It is a colourless gas with a choking, irritating smell, denser than air, and very soluble in water forming trioxosulphuric(IV) acid, a weak dibasic acid. It is an acidic oxide, a strong reducing agent that turns acidified potassium heptaoxodichromate(VI) from orange to green and acidified potassium tetraoxomanganate(VII) from purple to colourless, and it bleaches moist coloured materials such as flowers and straw by reduction, though the colour slowly returns on exposure to air. It is used as a bleaching agent for delicate materials, a fumigant and preservative, and above all in the manufacture of tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid.\n\nTetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, H2SO4, is manufactured by the contact process. It is a dense, oily, colourless liquid. Dilute acid shows typical acid properties, reacting with metals above hydrogen to give hydrogen, with bases to give salts, and with trioxocarbonates(IV) to give carbon(IV) oxide. Concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid has three additional characters. As a dehydrating agent it removes the elements of water from sugar, leaving a black mass of carbon, and from hydrated copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI), turning it white; it is therefore used to dry gases that do not react with it. As an oxidising agent it oxidises copper to copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) with the release of sulphur(IV) oxide, and oxidises carbon and sulphur. As a non-volatile acid it displaces more volatile acids from their salts, so it gives hydrogen chloride from sodium chloride and trioxonitrate(V) acid from a trioxonitrate(V). Great care is needed in dilution: the acid must always be added slowly to water with stirring, never water to acid, because the process is strongly exothermic. Uses include manufacture of fertilisers, detergents, paints, dyes, plastics, and as the electrolyte in lead-acid accumulators.\n\nHydrogen sulphide, H2S, prepared by the action of dilute acid on iron(II) sulphide, is a colourless gas smelling of rotten eggs, poisonous, weakly acidic in solution and a strong reducing agent; it precipitates coloured metal sulphides and is used in qualitative analysis. Tetraoxosulphates(VI) are detected by adding barium chloride solution acidified with dilute hydrochloric acid, which gives a white precipitate of barium tetraoxosulphate(VI) that is insoluble in excess acid.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES\n\nExample 1: Define allotropy and distinguish between rhombic and monoclinic sulphur.\nSolution: Allotropy is the existence of an element in two or more different forms in the same physical state, the forms differing in crystal structure and physical properties but not in chemical properties. Rhombic sulphur has octahedral crystals, a density of 2.06 g per cm3, a melting point of 113 degrees Celsius and is stable below 96 degrees Celsius. Monoclinic sulphur has needle-shaped crystals, a density of 1.98 g per cm3, a melting point of 119 degrees Celsius and is stable between 96 degrees Celsius and its melting point. The transition temperature between them is 96 degrees Celsius.\n\nExample 2: Explain the observation when concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid is added to sugar crystals.\nSolution: The white sugar rapidly turns yellow, then brown, and finally swells up into a spongy black mass with the release of steam and much heat. The concentrated acid acts as a dehydrating agent, removing hydrogen and oxygen from the sucrose in the ratio in which they occur in water. The equation may be written C12H22O11 giving 12C plus 11H2O. The residue is carbon, and the reaction shows that dehydration is different from simply drying a substance.\n\nExample 3: Describe a test to distinguish between sulphur(IV) oxide and carbon(IV) oxide, both of which turn lime water milky.\nSolution: Pass each gas separately through acidified potassium heptaoxodichromate(VI) solution on a filter paper or in a test tube. Sulphur(IV) oxide is a strong reducing agent and changes the orange colour to green as chromium(VI) is reduced to chromium(III). Carbon(IV) oxide produces no colour change because it has no reducing power. The pungent, choking smell of sulphur(IV) oxide, compared with the odourless carbon(IV) oxide, provides further confirmation.\n\nExample 4: Explain why concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid cannot be used to dry ammonia or hydrogen sulphide.\nSolution: A drying agent must not react chemically with the gas being dried. Ammonia is a base and reacts with the acid to form ammonium tetraoxosulphate(VI), so the gas would simply be absorbed. Hydrogen sulphide is a strong reducing agent and is oxidised by the concentrated acid to sulphur, which appears as a yellow deposit, while the acid is reduced to sulphur(IV) oxide. Ammonia is therefore dried with quicklime and hydrogen sulphide with phosphorus(V) oxide or anhydrous calcium chloride.\n\nExample 5: 5.6 g of iron was heated with excess sulphur. Calculate the mass of iron(II) sulphide formed. (Fe equals 56, S equals 32.)\nSolution: The equation is Fe plus S giving FeS, a 1 to 1 mole ratio. Moles of iron equal 5.6 divided by 56, which is 0.1 mol, so 0.1 mol of iron(II) sulphide is formed. The molar mass of FeS is 56 plus 32, that is 88 g per mole. The mass formed is therefore 0.1 times 88, which equals 8.8 g.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Sulphur has atomic number 16 and configuration 2, 8, 6.\n- Sulphur occurs free in volcanic regions and combined as sulphides and sulphates.\n- Galena, zinc blende and iron pyrites are important sulphide ores.\n- Sulphur is a yellow, brittle, non-metallic solid.\n- Sulphur is insoluble in water but soluble in carbon disulphide.\n- Allotropy is the existence of an element in different forms in the same state.\n- Rhombic sulphur is stable below 96 degrees Celsius.\n- Monoclinic sulphur is stable between 96 and 119 degrees Celsius.\n- The transition temperature of sulphur is 96 degrees Celsius.\n- Rhombic sulphur has octahedral crystals and higher density.\n- Monoclinic sulphur has needle-shaped crystals.\n- Plastic sulphur is amorphous and reverts to rhombic sulphur.\n- Sulphur burns in air with a blue flame to give sulphur(IV) oxide.\n- Sulphur combines directly with metals to form sulphides.\n- Sulphur is used in vulcanising rubber and making gunpowder.\n- Sulphur(IV) oxide is prepared by burning sulphur in air.\n- Sulphur(IV) oxide has a choking, irritating smell.\n- Sulphur(IV) oxide dissolves in water to give trioxosulphuric(IV) acid.\n- Sulphur(IV) oxide is a strong reducing agent.\n- Sulphur(IV) oxide turns acidified heptaoxodichromate(VI) orange to green.\n- Sulphur(IV) oxide bleaches by reduction and the colour returns.\n- Chlorine bleaches by oxidation and the bleaching is permanent.\n- Sulphur(IV) oxide is used as a preservative and fumigant.\n- Tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid is manufactured by the contact process.\n- Vanadium(V) oxide catalyses the contact process.\n- Sulphur(VI) oxide is absorbed in concentrated acid to give oleum.\n- Dilute tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid shows normal acid reactions.\n- Concentrated acid acts as a dehydrating agent.\n- Concentrated acid chars sugar to carbon.\n- Concentrated acid acts as an oxidising agent when hot.\n- Hot concentrated acid oxidises copper with release of sulphur(IV) oxide.\n- Concentrated acid displaces more volatile acids from their salts.\n- Acid must be added to water during dilution, never the reverse.\n- Tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid is used in accumulators and fertilisers.\n- Hydrogen sulphide smells of rotten eggs and is poisonous.\n- Hydrogen sulphide is a strong reducing agent.\n- Hydrogen sulphide precipitates coloured metal sulphides in analysis.\n- Barium chloride and dilute acid give a white precipitate with tetraoxosulphates(VI).\n- Trioxosulphates(IV) give sulphur(IV) oxide with dilute acid.',
